(228095, 12912, 1280, '<td valign="top">I based this image on the story in the Bible when Peter denied Jesus three times.  He never thought he would, but Jesus had prophesied that he would. I can imagine the despair he felt, when he realized he had indeed denied him.<br/>I cut out a cross from cardboard, and used a carving of a person in a position that, to me, looked humbled, and sorry.  <br/>I set this up on my table top with a white board as a background.  I used a clamp on lamp and put red cellophane in front of it. (the wrapping from a box of valentine candy.  I set my camera on a tripod, and shot a few rounds.</td>', 'I based this image on the story in the Bible when Peter denied Jesus three times.  He never thought he would, but Jesus had prophesied that he would. I can imagine the despair he felt, when he realized he had indeed denied him.I cut out a cross from cardboard, and used a carving of a person in a position that, to me, looked humbled, and sorry.  I set this up on my table top with a white board as a background.  I used a clamp on lamp and put red cellophane in front of it. (the wrapping from a box of valentine candy.  I set my camera on a tripod, and shot a few rounds.', '2003-03-03 19:21:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(228180, 12886, 5007, '<td valign="top">I would like to know if there is any way we can be of help. Is there an organization that could use contributions?</td>', 'I would like to know if there is any way we can be of help. Is there an organization that could use contributions?', '2003-03-03 20:12:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(228343, 13111, 3205, '<td valign="top">I am not real sure what caused the screen pattern that everyone saw.  No matter how much I used unsharp mask, it would not go away.  I almost did not submit this photograph for that reason, but I liked the photograph despite the pattern, and felt that it added to the overall mood of the photograph.  This structure is part of a large unused industrial complex, that had many great subjects for the challenge, but unfortunately, the security guard awoke from his nap before I could get to the other subjects, and chased me off!! <br/>Thank you for all your comments.</td>', 'I am not real sure what caused the screen pattern that everyone saw.  No matter how much I used unsharp mask, it would not go away.  I almost did not submit this photograph for that reason, but I liked the photograph despite the pattern, and felt that it added to the overall mood of the photograph.  This structure is part of a large unused industrial complex, that had many great subjects for the challenge, but unfortunately, the security guard awoke from his nap before I could get to the other subjects, and chased me off!! Thank you for all your comments.', '2003-03-03 22:30:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(228360, 13056, 4159, '<td valign="top">Thank you all for commenting.  I did indeed have a cold, wet butt.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-03-03 22:43:20.</i></td>', 'Thank you all for commenting.  I did indeed have a cold, wet butt.', '2003-03-03 22:42:58.000000', '2003-03-03 22:43:20.000000', 0),
(228386, 12951, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review<br/> <br/>Black and White is definetly the choice here.   Very good exposure, the snow is not washed out, and the stone is very well rendered.  I think you might want to consider a slightly shallower depth of field.  A little softer focus on the distant graves would still give a sense of place, yet not compete for attention as they do just a bit here.  As the picture appears now, it seems as if the stone is shot at an angle asking the viewer to look past it.  The only other downside is that there is not a real telling of the subject, despair.  All passing is with some sadness.  Well except for Hitler maybe.  But not all is with despair.  Was this person cut down before life could be lived to the fullest, or was it an extremely elderly person who had lived a very full life, and now it seems for the best is allowed to pass on.<br/> <br/>A model to pose as a mourner, could be very effective here.</td>', 'Critique Club Review Black and White is definetly the choice here.   Very good exposure, the snow is not washed out, and the stone is very well rendered.  I think you might want to consider a slightly shallower depth of field.  A little softer focus on the distant graves would still give a sense of place, yet not compete for attention as they do just a bit here.  As the picture appears now, it seems as if the stone is shot at an angle asking the viewer to look past it.  The only other downside is that there is not a real telling of the subject, despair.  All passing is with some sadness.  Well except for Hitler maybe.  But not all is with despair.  Was this person cut down before life could be lived to the fullest, or was it an extremely elderly person who had lived a very full life, and now it seems for the best is allowed to pass on. A model to pose as a mourner, could be very effective here.', '2003-03-03 22:54:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(228396, 13100, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review<br/> <br/>Very good focus and depth of field.  Interesting lighting.  Very good, but a bit of washout at the base of the neck.  With such a severe angle on the lighting, the fingers leave the face in darkness.  Perhaps this was a goal for a subtle message, but it also hides the look of despair.  With the lighting and angle, this could also be something out of the Aliens movies where they get grabbed by the face.  Despair or assault?  I think a second, low key light that revealed more of the expression behind the hand, could have been the ticket here.</td>', 'Critique Club Review Very good focus and depth of field.  Interesting lighting.  Very good, but a bit of washout at the base of the neck.  With such a severe angle on the lighting, the fingers leave the face in darkness.  Perhaps this was a goal for a subtle message, but it also hides the look of despair.  With the lighting and angle, this could also be something out of the Aliens movies where they get grabbed by the face.  Despair or assault?  I think a second, low key light that revealed more of the expression behind the hand, could have been the ticket here.', '2003-03-03 22:59:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(228481, 13085, 5212, '<td valign="top">Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Meeting the Challenge:  Does this well, the model and photograph "planner" did well illustrating this emotion at the focal point of the image.<br/> <br/>Composition:  Would have liked to see a tighter crop, eliminating the furniture, and cords behind the girl.  I think it would have added some extra drama to the shot.  I like the way the girls arm leads to the letter.<br/> <br/>Technical Quality:  I am not one that prefers graininess in any photos unless they are intended to be abstract (and as I said, that''s my personal preference).  There are a lot of jpg artifacts to the right of the girl, and by the desk, which are unattractive.  I do like the darkness of the focal point, helping to emphasize the dark mood.<br/> <br/>Creativity:  I think you did well with this at the focal point. Even though the photo is staged, you put a lot of effort into all the details with the subject, good job.<br/> <br/>Overall Conclusion:  As noted above, a tighter crop and increased photo quality would have helped this shot a lot, although the message is right on.  <br/> <br/>If you have questions, feel free to email me!<br/> <br/>Kandice Jones</td>', 'Critique Club: Meeting the Challenge:  Does this well, the model and photograph "planner" did well illustrating this emotion at the focal point of the image. Composition:  Would have liked to see a tighter crop, eliminating the furniture, and cords behind the girl.  I think it would have added some extra drama to the shot.  I like the way the girls arm leads to the letter. Technical Quality:  I am not one that prefers graininess in any photos unless they are intended to be abstract (and as I said, that''s my personal preference).  There are a lot of jpg artifacts to the right of the girl, and by the desk, which are unattractive.  I do like the darkness of the focal point, helping to emphasize the dark mood. Creativity:  I think you did well with this at the focal point. Even though the photo is staged, you put a lot of effort into all the details with the subject, good job. Overall Conclusion:  As noted above, a tighter crop and increased photo quality would have helped this shot a lot, although the message is right on.   If you have questions, feel free to email me! Kandice Jones', '2003-03-03 23:49:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(228501, 12818, 5212, '<td valign="top">Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Meeting the Challenge:  Very well done.  Any mom (or parent) should appreciate this look of desparation, even though some of the voter''s didn''t.<br/> <br/>Composition and technical: I think that this may be a little problematic.  <br/> <br/>The angle of the shot and the cropping makes her arm and body look really small in comparison to her head, and maybe her forehead a little proportionally large. <br/> <br/>There is a little light spot in the upper left that is a bit distracting.  A little rotation and tighter crop could have eliminated some of your problems. and intensified the subject.  <br/> <br/>I also like to see the eyes more at 2/3rds up the photo rather than in the middle, although I know this was definitely not a posed shot for this difficult challenge.  <br/> <br/>The B/W works well to add to the mood of this photo.<br/> <br/>The angle of the shot and lighting makes the eyes look quite a bit different even though the lighting on either side of the face doesn''t look that extremely different.  I have this same problem with eyes when I take pictures of our cats (our kids are grown, so the cats are our ''babies''!). <br/> <br/>Also, as noted below, the skin almost looks too flat, but it also looks like you used a flash for this shot.  You might try softening the flash by putting something over it to soften it down a little.  Sometimes I have used a nylon hose or a makeshift bounce from a card over mine to help spread it out.  It would have helped with that shadow on her shoulder, which adds to the "smallness" of her body.<br/> <br/>Overall:  Excellent idea for the challenge, and good candid photo of this ill child.  Needed a little technical improvement.  HOpe I haven''t been too critical, but also wanted to try to be helpful.  If you have any questions, please feel free to email me.  Kandice</td>', 'Critique Club: Meeting the Challenge:  Very well done.  Any mom (or parent) should appreciate this look of desparation, even though some of the voter''s didn''t. Composition and technical: I think that this may be a little problematic.   The angle of the shot and the cropping makes her arm and body look really small in comparison to her head, and maybe her forehead a little proportionally large.  There is a little light spot in the upper left that is a bit distracting.  A little rotation and tighter crop could have eliminated some of your problems. and intensified the subject.   I also like to see the eyes more at 2/3rds up the photo rather than in the middle, although I know this was definitely not a posed shot for this difficult challenge.   The B/W works well to add to the mood of this photo. The angle of the shot and lighting makes the eyes look quite a bit different even though the lighting on either side of the face doesn''t look that extremely different.  I have this same problem with eyes when I take pictures of our cats (our kids are grown, so the cats are our ''babies''!).  Also, as noted below, the skin almost looks too flat, but it also looks like you used a flash for this shot.  You might try softening the flash by putting something over it to soften it down a little.  Sometimes I have used a nylon hose or a makeshift bounce from a card over mine to help spread it out.  It would have helped with that shadow on her shoulder, which adds to the "smallness" of her body. Overall:  Excellent idea for the challenge, and good candid photo of this ill child.  Needed a little technical improvement.  HOpe I haven''t been too critical, but also wanted to try to be helpful.  If you have any questions, please feel free to email me.  Kandice', '2003-03-04 00:03:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(228669, 12790, 6285, '<td valign="top">Not so much the fear of death, but more that the stones have no names and no faces anymore.  This is where I want to be buried actually.<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by byronb:</b></div><hr/><i>nice image. I guess if you fear death it could be a despair photo, this rather looks like a place I wouldn''t mind being buried in. Your thoughts?</i></td></tr></table></td>', 'Not so much the fear of death, but more that the stones have no names and no faces anymore.  This is where I want to be buried actually. Originally posted by byronb:nice image. I guess if you fear death it could be a despair photo, this rather looks like a place I wouldn''t mind being buried in. Your thoughts?', '2003-03-04 08:24:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(228713, 12886, 5792, '<td valign="top">With your image and comments you have awakened many to the plight of the homeless in your country. You have done a great service with your image, much more than just winning a photo contest.<br/> <br/>Congratulations. Yours was a well deserved win in this very difficult category. I was pleased that at lease two of the top three were voted for the category. This was tough because so many images were staged, but voters came through once again making the right choice.<br/> <br/>Thank you.   <br/> <br/> <br/> <br/></td>', 'With your image and comments you have awakened many to the plight of the homeless in your country. You have done a great service with your image, much more than just winning a photo contest. Congratulations. Yours was a well deserved win in this very difficult category. I was pleased that at lease two of the top three were voted for the category. This was tough because so many images were staged, but voters came through once again making the right choice. Thank you.      ', '2003-03-04 09:37:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(228715, 12962, 5792, '<td valign="top">You have captured the despair of the homeless in this country.<br/> <br/>Your choice of sepia and content is outstanding. You are deserving of your placing. <br/> <br/>You captured a moment that silences those who say the homeless choose that as their personal lifestyle. This image says it best. You are to be commended.</td>', 'You have captured the despair of the homeless in this country. Your choice of sepia and content is outstanding. You are deserving of your placing.  You captured a moment that silences those who say the homeless choose that as their personal lifestyle. This image says it best. You are to be commended.', '2003-03-04 09:45:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(228856, 13027, 2764, '<td valign="top">Okay - now I''m back.  I''ll be writing this at work in bits and pieces-  I have to save as I go along because I''ve lost too many reviews to computer glitches.  I''ll lwt you know when I am all done.<br/> <br/>Hello from the Critique CLub-  I like your work and I''m glad to have the chance to review another of your pictures-  Funny that they are both green and both sad and both sort of bleak.  This interminable winter must be getting to you.<br/> <br/>I really like this photo.  Yes it is bleak, yes this conveys despair, challenge well met.   I love the solitary mood, just the guy and his shadow.  What does the shadow represent?  All his fears and disappointments that share this lonely room with him?  I like a picture that tells a story.<br/> <br/>Someone on DPC recently said that photography is all about light.   Your dramatic use of light and shadow is the best part of this picture.  Although the light appears to come from a window, the window itself doesn''t exist in the mood of the room.  The light shines directly on the figure but in a way seems to pass him by and focus on his shadow.  The high contrast lighting on the mans arms and legs make him an abstact shape.  His face and his..um..other parts are hidden in shadow.  That makes the person anonymous and leaves his pain as the main subject of the photo.  So what is the central focus here?  Maybe his hand, the one with the wrist watch?  That''s why I think he might be ready to punch someone, if he can overcome the weight of his emotions.<br/> <br/>I especially like the composition of this shot.  <br/>1.  The subject is backed up into the upper right corner.   The whole of the bottom half is filled with interesting patterns of light and dark.  Did you rake the carpet to be just so?  The dark foreground seems to be creeping up on our man, stopped for a moment by the brighter circle of carpet by his feet.  Will that light be enough to stop him from getting engulfed by the darkness?  I don''t think so, the carpet waves are moving towards him.<br/>2.  The upper left corner is empty and only serves as a place for those two wonderful leading lines, the double shadow that points right to the subject.  So the foreground creep up on him and the Leftground (not a real word, I made iit up) points at him accusingly,  poor fellow, even his room of refuge is against him.<br/>3.  The man himself, all folded up is a nice study of crisscrossing lines.  His shadow behind him repeats his shape in silhouette.<br/> <br/>Another effective element is your textures.  The fuzzy carpet leads to the furry man and is reflected in the graininess of the walls.  Nice.   The grainy look works really well here.<br/> <br/>Why does our friend look so tense and uncomfortable (I mean aside from the fact that he''s naked in front of a camera)?  Maybe he is incredibly angry as well as sad.  His arms are squeezing tight to his legs.  His head is pushed down, not fallen down.  Even his toes are tense.  He seems to be spring loaded with tension.  I think his attitude makes the scene more interesting.  it combines with the shadows and pointing lines to imply that something (maybe bad) is going to happen.  It is certainly not a static picture of rejection or hopelessness.<br/> <br/>I changed my mind about the wrist watch. I like it.  I decided that it adds to the mood rather than detracts from it.  THis is a modern scene in a modern apartment.  The carpet and woodwork date the image t the present and so the watch is in keeping.  I makes a statement about our times..the pressure of time?...too much to do?....time running out for him??..all sorts of possibilities.<br/> <br/>I have trouble making suggestions on pictures that are near perfect.  One little thing, now that the challenge is over, I think you should go back and clone out some of the pnael lines on the door.  One of them makes his shadow have a funny square corner.  Also, if it looks like a door it suggests a hint of a way out.  Do you want him to have that glimmer of hope or not?  Although I like it in the monotone color you have chosen, I''d be curious to see it in black and white as some commenters suggested.<br/> <br/> <br/>I always put a disclaimer:  Please remember that this is just my opinion and I am definaely NOT an expert.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-03-14 15:20:05.</i></td>', 'Okay - now I''m back.  I''ll be writing this at work in bits and pieces-  I have to save as I go along because I''ve lost too many reviews to computer glitches.  I''ll lwt you know when I am all done. Hello from the Critique CLub-  I like your work and I''m glad to have the chance to review another of your pictures-  Funny that they are both green and both sad and both sort of bleak.  This interminable winter must be getting to you. I really like this photo.  Yes it is bleak, yes this conveys despair, challenge well met.   I love the solitary mood, just the guy and his shadow.  What does the shadow represent?  All his fears and disappointments that share this lonely room with him?  I like a picture that tells a story. Someone on DPC recently said that photography is all about light.   Your dramatic use of light and shadow is the best part of this picture.  Although the light appears to come from a window, the window itself doesn''t exist in the mood of the room.  The light shines directly on the figure but in a way seems to pass him by and focus on his shadow.  The high contrast lighting on the mans arms and legs make him an abstact shape.  His face and his..um..other parts are hidden in shadow.  That makes the person anonymous and leaves his pain as the main subject of the photo.  So what is the central focus here?  Maybe his hand, the one with the wrist watch?  That''s why I think he might be ready to punch someone, if he can overcome the weight of his emotions. I especially like the composition of this shot.  1.  The subject is backed up into the upper right corner.   The whole of the bottom half is filled with interesting patterns of light and dark.  Did you rake the carpet to be just so?  The dark foreground seems to be creeping up on our man, stopped for a moment by the brighter circle of carpet by his feet.  Will that light be enough to stop him from getting engulfed by the darkness?  I don''t think so, the carpet waves are moving towards him.2.  The upper left corner is empty and only serves as a place for those two wonderful leading lines, the double shadow that points right to the subject.  So the foreground creep up on him and the Leftground (not a real word, I made iit up) points at him accusingly,  poor fellow, even his room of refuge is against him.3.  The man himself, all folded up is a nice study of crisscrossing lines.  His shadow behind him repeats his shape in silhouette. Another effective element is your textures.  The fuzzy carpet leads to the furry man and is reflected in the graininess of the walls.  Nice.   The grainy look works really well here. Why does our friend look so tense and uncomfortable (I mean aside from the fact that he''s naked in front of a camera)?  Maybe he is incredibly angry as well as sad.  His arms are squeezing tight to his legs.  His head is pushed down, not fallen down.  Even his toes are tense.  He seems to be spring loaded with tension.  I think his attitude makes the scene more interesting.  it combines with the shadows and pointing lines to imply that something (maybe bad) is going to happen.  It is certainly not a static picture of rejection or hopelessness. I changed my mind about the wrist watch. I like it.  I decided that it adds to the mood rather than detracts from it.  THis is a modern scene in a modern apartment.  The carpet and woodwork date the image t the present and so the watch is in keeping.  I makes a statement about our times..the pressure of time?...too much to do?....time running out for him??..all sorts of possibilities. I have trouble making suggestions on pictures that are near perfect.  One little thing, now that the challenge is over, I think you should go back and clone out some of the pnael lines on the door.  One of them makes his shadow have a funny square corner.  Also, if it looks like a door it suggests a hint of a way out.  Do you want him to have that glimmer of hope or not?  Although I like it in the monotone color you have chosen, I''d be curious to see it in black and white as some commenters suggested.  I always put a disclaimer:  Please remember that this is just my opinion and I am definaely NOT an expert.', '2003-03-04 13:02:55.000000', '2003-03-14 15:20:05.000000', 0),
(228901, 12962, 2079, '<td valign="top">Thank you everyone for taking time to comment. There were a lot of great entries in this challenge - Thank you for the ribbon!</td>', 'Thank you everyone for taking time to comment. There were a lot of great entries in this challenge - Thank you for the ribbon!', '2003-03-04 13:42:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(229012, 13095, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique CLub Comments by Grayce<br/>This is a cute and expressive picture.  The child is adorable, and I can see she is trying hard to reach waaaaaaaaay up. I see determination, and frustration, but not really despair.  <br/>Technically this is good.  The focus is excellent and the colors are very good.  Your framing and crop are fine. Nice that you didn''t center the child.  Also good that you included that curved slide.<br/>If I took this picture, I''d be proud of it.  If it was my child I''d cherish it.  Great action shot.<br/>Overall good job.  I see no problems at all.<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique CLub Comments by GrayceThis is a cute and expressive picture.  The child is adorable, and I can see she is trying hard to reach waaaaaaaaay up. I see determination, and frustration, but not really despair.  Technically this is good.  The focus is excellent and the colors are very good.  Your framing and crop are fine. Nice that you didn''t center the child.  Also good that you included that curved slide.If I took this picture, I''d be proud of it.  If it was my child I''d cherish it.  Great action shot.Overall good job.  I see no problems at all.Regards, Grayce', '2003-03-04 15:38:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(229029, 13072, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club COmments by Grayce<br/>Karma,<br/>This is a lovely image, and black and white suits it well. For many years I lived in the mountains, and I remember how slippery the mountain roads could be, and treacherous with limited visibility.  This provokes recollections of the feelings I had, when I had to drive in it.  <br/>I wouldn''t have thought of it for despair, but ya know what?  It works!<br/>The composition works well with the roadway being on a diagonal and leading into the distance.  The trees lining the road support that.<br/>Focus is fine for the type of picture....fog, rain, mist.  None of it is crystal clear.<br/>OVerall I like this. Very moody.  Good job!<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique Club COmments by GrayceKarma,This is a lovely image, and black and white suits it well. For many years I lived in the mountains, and I remember how slippery the mountain roads could be, and treacherous with limited visibility.  This provokes recollections of the feelings I had, when I had to drive in it.  I wouldn''t have thought of it for despair, but ya know what?  It works!The composition works well with the roadway being on a diagonal and leading into the distance.  The trees lining the road support that.Focus is fine for the type of picture....fog, rain, mist.  None of it is crystal clear.OVerall I like this. Very moody.  Good job!Regards, Grayce', '2003-03-04 15:56:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(229138, 12933, 4712, '<td valign="top">Susi did a wonderful job, congrats to her as well.  Its hard to find good models/girlfriends these days.</td>', 'Susi did a wonderful job, congrats to her as well.  Its hard to find good models/girlfriends these days.', '2003-03-04 18:44:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(229145, 13097, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Achiral...<br/> <br/>I really love the way these chess pieces absorb the light.  I have one of these sets myself and have made quite a few photos with it so far.  <br/> <br/>I understand your theme of ''despair'' in this shot since the King is definitely in dire straits.  Doom is imminent and the game will soon be over.  <br/> <br/>Black and white was also an excellent choice for this photograph and your exposure is pretty good.  The lighting in the frosted glass pieces is just a tad hot, but otherwise great exposure.<br/> <br/>With the theme you have chosen for your despair shot, I would have done one thing differently knowing what I know about these chess pieces.  Since the theme of your shot is ''despair'' and the solo King is the piece in trouble, I would have used the frosted glass King as the object piece and the clear glass pieces as the attackers in this shot.  The frosted glass King would have become a much greater focal point as it absorbs and distributes the light much better than the clear glass pieces.  The secondary clear glass pieces would have become less of a focal point and really done a nice job of accenting your King in despair :)<br/> <br/>Keep up the good work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Achiral... I really love the way these chess pieces absorb the light.  I have one of these sets myself and have made quite a few photos with it so far.   I understand your theme of ''despair'' in this shot since the King is definitely in dire straits.  Doom is imminent and the game will soon be over.   Black and white was also an excellent choice for this photograph and your exposure is pretty good.  The lighting in the frosted glass pieces is just a tad hot, but otherwise great exposure. With the theme you have chosen for your despair shot, I would have done one thing differently knowing what I know about these chess pieces.  Since the theme of your shot is ''despair'' and the solo King is the piece in trouble, I would have used the frosted glass King as the object piece and the clear glass pieces as the attackers in this shot.  The frosted glass King would have become a much greater focal point as it absorbs and distributes the light much better than the clear glass pieces.  The secondary clear glass pieces would have become less of a focal point and really done a nice job of accenting your King in despair :) Keep up the good work :) John Setzler', '2003-03-04 18:52:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(229318, 12918, 5475, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club!  First of all, welcome back!  I saw that you didn''t have any submissions for about six months.  Glad you are back and participating!<br/> <br/>I like the use of long time exposures and I like that you were adventurous enough to try something like this.<br/> <br/>With respect to showing despair...<br/>I like the body language here.  The hand on the left knee says ''stress'' to me.  The lowered head and closed eyes indicates that he is in deep thought, possibly agonizing over something, maybe some regrets??  I like the fact that the image shows two positions.  The second appears a little staged, but it gets the point across.  The fact that there are two images kind of tells a story.  The first image is a beginning, and the second progresses to ''despair'' (or at least distraught to a certain extent!).  <br/>I like that this shot is taken outside.  It helps add to the despair theme, possibly indicating that this person wanted to get away and be alone.<br/>I like the fact that there are some background details instead of an all dark background.  I don''t think I like the way the entry steps overlap his head(s).  I like the fact that some details are there, but I would have liked to see the steps off to the left or right of his head.<br/>Concerning the ''judiciously'' applied dust and scratches filter...<br/>The effect is applied in a manner that it borders on the effects you''d get with the watercolor or cutout filters in Photoshop.  My instinct is to say I feel this was overdone a little.  It would be interesting to see the original image before this effect was applied.  Most challenges require technical excellence, but I think this topic of despair works well with dark, grainy images.    <br/> <br/>Overall a good image.  I like the fact that you experimented and tried something unusual.  Congrats for your creativity, although acting lessons may be in order to truly portray despair.  ..trying to imply despair sure beats actually having despair, though!  Keep plugging away and earn yourself that Canon D60!!<br/> <br/>JD Anderson</td>', 'From the Critique Club!  First of all, welcome back!  I saw that you didn''t have any submissions for about six months.  Glad you are back and participating! I like the use of long time exposures and I like that you were adventurous enough to try something like this. With respect to showing despair...I like the body language here.  The hand on the left knee says ''stress'' to me.  The lowered head and closed eyes indicates that he is in deep thought, possibly agonizing over something, maybe some regrets??  I like the fact that the image shows two positions.  The second appears a little staged, but it gets the point across.  The fact that there are two images kind of tells a story.  The first image is a beginning, and the second progresses to ''despair'' (or at least distraught to a certain extent!).  I like that this shot is taken outside.  It helps add to the despair theme, possibly indicating that this person wanted to get away and be alone.I like the fact that there are some background details instead of an all dark background.  I don''t think I like the way the entry steps overlap his head(s).  I like the fact that some details are there, but I would have liked to see the steps off to the left or right of his head.Concerning the ''judiciously'' applied dust and scratches filter...The effect is applied in a manner that it borders on the effects you''d get with the watercolor or cutout filters in Photoshop.  My instinct is to say I feel this was overdone a little.  It would be interesting to see the original image before this effect was applied.  Most challenges require technical excellence, but I think this topic of despair works well with dark, grainy images.     Overall a good image.  I like the fact that you experimented and tried something unusual.  Congrats for your creativity, although acting lessons may be in order to truly portray despair.  ..trying to imply despair sure beats actually having despair, though!  Keep plugging away and earn yourself that Canon D60!! JD Anderson', '2003-03-04 22:33:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(229324, 13121, 5475, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>With regards to despair -- this image says more ''fear'' to me than it does despair, two very different emotions.  I wouldn''t lower your score, though, because this could result in despair afterwards  (ie, the victim''s widow would despair).<br/> <br/>At first glance, this is a powerful image.  It gets your attention right away!  After that initial reaction, I think some improvements could be made with the composition.  In general, I don''t like the main subject to be in the center of the frame.  I would have liked it better  if the barrel of the gun were more to the left and up a little (maybe in the upper left 1/3).  That would bring more of the hand into view.  I realize the end of the barrel is the main attraction here, but I feel the photo would have more balance if more of the hand were shown.  It seems ''cutoff'' to me.<br/>I really like your choice of focus and depth of field.  As I said earlier, the end of the barrel is the main attraction, and your choice of focus and DOF really succeeds here.  Lighting on the end of the gun is perfect.  I also feel you have just the right amount of light on the hand.  I''d like to see a little more light on the right side of the gun--not very much, just a little.  Maybe a reflector card rather than another light source.<br/> <br/>I like the black background, and the shape of your composition.  It is a nice use of negative space.<br/> <br/>I hope you find these comments helpful.  Good luck in future challenges!<br/>JD Anderson</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! With regards to despair -- this image says more ''fear'' to me than it does despair, two very different emotions.  I wouldn''t lower your score, though, because this could result in despair afterwards  (ie, the victim''s widow would despair). At first glance, this is a powerful image.  It gets your attention right away!  After that initial reaction, I think some improvements could be made with the composition.  In general, I don''t like the main subject to be in the center of the frame.  I would have liked it better  if the barrel of the gun were more to the left and up a little (maybe in the upper left 1/3).  That would bring more of the hand into view.  I realize the end of the barrel is the main attraction here, but I feel the photo would have more balance if more of the hand were shown.  It seems ''cutoff'' to me.I really like your choice of focus and depth of field.  As I said earlier, the end of the barrel is the main attraction, and your choice of focus and DOF really succeeds here.  Lighting on the end of the gun is perfect.  I also feel you have just the right amount of light on the hand.  I''d like to see a little more light on the right side of the gun--not very much, just a little.  Maybe a reflector card rather than another light source. I like the black background, and the shape of your composition.  It is a nice use of negative space. I hope you find these comments helpful.  Good luck in future challenges!JD Anderson', '2003-03-04 22:49:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(229663, 12930, 1024, '<td valign="top">Comment: <br/>Critique Club Critique <br/> <br/>(1) COMPOSITION (CONTENT) This is a wonderful photograph. The composition is right on, and adds a real power to the feeling being displayed.  The blue color cast adds to this as well.  The grainy-ness almost give the appearance of being underwater, they could pass as bubbles. <br/> <br/>(2) BACKGROUND The background is very interesting.  It gives the photograph a very textured feeling. The leading lines from the background lead right to the face and especially the open mouth.... very strong emotion.<br/> <br/>(3) CAMERA WORK ,TECHNICAL I love how you created this photograph.  The overall dispair is so evident.   <br/> <br/>(4) DIGITAL PROCESSING ,TECHNICAL Your post processing is very good. Very effective cropping, leaving only the important details in the photograph.<br/> <br/>(5)MEETING THE CHALLENGE You have done a marvalous job of meeting the challenge in an original and refreshing way. <br/> <br/>(6) MY OPINION ON THE PHOTO The subject here is wonderful. The way you executed it is great. This was one of the best of the dispair pics. I do not understand why this did not score better.  Many times the most creative photos are not appreciated by the masses.</td>', 'Comment: Critique Club Critique  (1) COMPOSITION (CONTENT) This is a wonderful photograph. The composition is right on, and adds a real power to the feeling being displayed.  The blue color cast adds to this as well.  The grainy-ness almost give the appearance of being underwater, they could pass as bubbles.  (2) BACKGROUND The background is very interesting.  It gives the photograph a very textured feeling. The leading lines from the background lead right to the face and especially the open mouth.... very strong emotion. (3) CAMERA WORK ,TECHNICAL I love how you created this photograph.  The overall dispair is so evident.    (4) DIGITAL PROCESSING ,TECHNICAL Your post processing is very good. Very effective cropping, leaving only the important details in the photograph. (5)MEETING THE CHALLENGE You have done a marvalous job of meeting the challenge in an original and refreshing way.  (6) MY OPINION ON THE PHOTO The subject here is wonderful. The way you executed it is great. This was one of the best of the dispair pics. I do not understand why this did not score better.  Many times the most creative photos are not appreciated by the masses.', '2003-03-05 12:25:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(229693, 12831, 1024, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Critique <br/> <br/>(1) COMPOSITION (CONTENT) This is a great photograph. I love the the compositional use of framing of her face with her hair and her arms. I am a little bothered that you have cut off part of her head and part of her arm, but maybe that tight crop added some strength to the photograph.  (I would like to see it both ways to be sure).<br/> <br/>I have read some of your comments regarding the lip gloss.  I have to agree that this is the one main distraction.  <br/> <br/>(2) BACKGROUND There is not much background here, and so nothing to distract from the subject.<br/> <br/>(3) CAMERA WORK ,TECHNICAL The DOF is very good, all of the important parts are in sharp focus. Exposure is right on.  I notice that your exposure time was one second.  Nice decision to do it that way as opposed to on-camera-flash, which would have ruined this photograph.<br/> <br/>(4) DIGITAL PROCESSING ,TECHNICAL Your post processing is good. Wonderful use of duotone. Is very reminiscent of an old movie. <br/> <br/>(5)MEETING THE CHALLENGE You have done a fantastic job of meeting the challenge.  Your model is very good too.  I wonder how she managed to hold still for an entire second.  Its hard not to get movement in a photogrraph like this.<br/> <br/>(6) MY OPINION ON THE PHOTO The subject here is wonderful. The way you executed it is great. This was one of the best of the dispair pics. I am happy to see your score reflecting this. Well done!</td>', 'Critique Club Critique  (1) COMPOSITION (CONTENT) This is a great photograph. I love the the compositional use of framing of her face with her hair and her arms. I am a little bothered that you have cut off part of her head and part of her arm, but maybe that tight crop added some strength to the photograph.  (I would like to see it both ways to be sure). I have read some of your comments regarding the lip gloss.  I have to agree that this is the one main distraction.   (2) BACKGROUND There is not much background here, and so nothing to distract from the subject. (3) CAMERA WORK ,TECHNICAL The DOF is very good, all of the important parts are in sharp focus. Exposure is right on.  I notice that your exposure time was one second.  Nice decision to do it that way as opposed to on-camera-flash, which would have ruined this photograph. (4) DIGITAL PROCESSING ,TECHNICAL Your post processing is good. Wonderful use of duotone. Is very reminiscent of an old movie.  (5)MEETING THE CHALLENGE You have done a fantastic job of meeting the challenge.  Your model is very good too.  I wonder how she managed to hold still for an entire second.  Its hard not to get movement in a photogrraph like this. (6) MY OPINION ON THE PHOTO The subject here is wonderful. The way you executed it is great. This was one of the best of the dispair pics. I am happy to see your score reflecting this. Well done!', '2003-03-05 13:06:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(229777, 12930, 5982, '<td valign="top">Wow, you made my day!  Thank you so much and thanks for taking the time to comment.<br/> <br/>Nathalie</td>', 'Wow, you made my day!  Thank you so much and thanks for taking the time to comment. Nathalie', '2003-03-05 13:53:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(229851, 12771, 3599, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>Reading your own comments this photo would meet the challenge to me.  But because those aren''t visible during voting many didn''t get the connection.  Maybe a different title than the one you had as Beauty in Ice doesn''t convey despair.  I can''t think of another title that may worked though. This is a nice macro shot of the Dogwood bud.  There is some noise pixelation in the photo.  If you can, try a program like Neatimage to clean it up a bit.  Sorry that this didn''t do better and I hope you''ll still participate in future challenges.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club. Reading your own comments this photo would meet the challenge to me.  But because those aren''t visible during voting many didn''t get the connection.  Maybe a different title than the one you had as Beauty in Ice doesn''t convey despair.  I can''t think of another title that may worked though. This is a nice macro shot of the Dogwood bud.  There is some noise pixelation in the photo.  If you can, try a program like Neatimage to clean it up a bit.  Sorry that this didn''t do better and I hope you''ll still participate in future challenges.', '2003-03-05 14:18:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(229890, 13050, 1041, '<td valign="top"><i>CC review continued ... for some reason I wasn''t able to paste my whole review into one window ...</i> <br/> <br/><b>(5) Meeting the Challenge</b> <br/>The feelings (other than love for cats ;) that come to mind when I look at this photo are more of loneliness and being lost rather than despair.  I don''t think animal photos are easily used to show despair, it''s more of a human feeling to me.  But that might just be me.<br/> <br/><b>(6) My Opinion</b> <br/>I have kind of mixed feelings about this photo.  I don''t think it fits the challenge, but it is a cute cat, and there are only minor things that I pointed out above that could be changed.  I think the main reason for me to have mixed feelings is because I liked your entry into the stock challenge much better, the whole image was happier (more appropriate in that challenge) and reflects the playfulness of kittens better.  Does that make sense?<br/> <br/>Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review.<br/> <br/>Franziska.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-03-05 15:38:04.</i></td>', 'CC review continued ... for some reason I wasn''t able to paste my whole review into one window ...  (5) Meeting the Challenge The feelings (other than love for cats ;) that come to mind when I look at this photo are more of loneliness and being lost rather than despair.  I don''t think animal photos are easily used to show despair, it''s more of a human feeling to me.  But that might just be me. (6) My Opinion I have kind of mixed feelings about this photo.  I don''t think it fits the challenge, but it is a cute cat, and there are only minor things that I pointed out above that could be changed.  I think the main reason for me to have mixed feelings is because I liked your entry into the stock challenge much better, the whole image was happier (more appropriate in that challenge) and reflects the playfulness of kittens better.  Does that make sense? Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review. Franziska.', '2003-03-05 15:36:14.000000', '2003-03-05 15:38:04.000000', 0),
(229896, 13050, 1041, '<td valign="top"><b>Critique Club Comment</b> <br/> <br/><b>First Impression</b> <br/>Such a cute kitten ... I adore cats.  Not looking too happy though.  Unusual color scheme for a cat photo, but I think it might just work.<br/> <br/><b>(1) Composition / Content</b> <br/>Good placement of the kitten up against the back of the sofa, and also within the photo, using the rule of thirds.  It works here, because it gives the kitten space to move into if it wanted to. This also emphasizes the feeling of isolation a bit.<br/> <br/><b>(2) Background</b> <br/>The background is very unobtrusive and turns this photo almost into a monochromatic image, all shades of brown.  The shadows break up the monotony which is nice.  <br/> <br/><b>(3) Camera Work / Technical</b> <br/>The picture is focused nicely, and not over- or underexposed.  The backlighting throws a shadow on the cat which is ok for the purpose of this photo (to depict despair), but in other situations I would prefer to see the light from the left, this could also add some catchlights to the kitten''s eyes, which I think would add to the photo.<br/> <br/><b>(4) Post-processing</b> <br/>I think this picture could do with just a little increase in contrast to give it some more "umph".  No signs of oversharpening or anything like that.  The color of the border works well in keeping with the color scheme of the whole photo, but I feel it is a little too heavy for a photo of such a small kitten.</td>', 'Critique Club Comment  First Impression Such a cute kitten ... I adore cats.  Not looking too happy though.  Unusual color scheme for a cat photo, but I think it might just work. (1) Composition / Content Good placement of the kitten up against the back of the sofa, and also within the photo, using the rule of thirds.  It works here, because it gives the kitten space to move into if it wanted to. This also emphasizes the feeling of isolation a bit. (2) Background The background is very unobtrusive and turns this photo almost into a monochromatic image, all shades of brown.  The shadows break up the monotony which is nice.   (3) Camera Work / Technical The picture is focused nicely, and not over- or underexposed.  The backlighting throws a shadow on the cat which is ok for the purpose of this photo (to depict despair), but in other situations I would prefer to see the light from the left, this could also add some catchlights to the kitten''s eyes, which I think would add to the photo. (4) Post-processing I think this picture could do with just a little increase in contrast to give it some more "umph".  No signs of oversharpening or anything like that.  The color of the border works well in keeping with the color scheme of the whole photo, but I feel it is a little too heavy for a photo of such a small kitten.', '2003-03-05 15:37:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(229915, 13056, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>I think this is an excellent photo and it seems to have scored very well also.  I really like the sharp contrast.  It seems to set the mood for ''despair'' very nicely in this image.  The feeling of loneliness is very strong in this image and that suppports the theme of ''despair'' as well.  There is just enough detail in the man''s face to really make this photo pop.  <br/> <br/>I don''t think I could offer you any suggestions for improvement on this photo because I like it just the way it is :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) I think this is an excellent photo and it seems to have scored very well also.  I really like the sharp contrast.  It seems to set the mood for ''despair'' very nicely in this image.  The feeling of loneliness is very strong in this image and that suppports the theme of ''despair'' as well.  There is just enough detail in the man''s face to really make this photo pop.   I don''t think I could offer you any suggestions for improvement on this photo because I like it just the way it is :) John Setzler', '2003-03-05 16:11:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(229924, 12965, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB:<br/> <br/>Challenge: Definitely meets the challenge.  The dog looks like he really does want out!<br/> <br/>Technique:  I love the tonal finish and the shadows in this shot.  You might want to set your focus on the dog next time, as the sharp focus on the fence is a little distracting. (the bright light brings the eye to it, instead of to the dog)<br/> <br/>Overall: A really great shot!  I am very impressed and intrigued with the shadows and lighting.  I think your focus is the only thing here that needs improving.</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB: Challenge: Definitely meets the challenge.  The dog looks like he really does want out! Technique:  I love the tonal finish and the shadows in this shot.  You might want to set your focus on the dog next time, as the sharp focus on the fence is a little distracting. (the bright light brings the eye to it, instead of to the dog) Overall: A really great shot!  I am very impressed and intrigued with the shadows and lighting.  I think your focus is the only thing here that needs improving.', '2003-03-05 16:51:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(229928, 12825, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>Challenge:  Definitely meets the challenge. I think it would have been a little more compelling with a tear or maybe more jagged writing.  Your writing looks so beautiful and "happy", not desperate. (that''s a backwards compliment)<br/> <br/>Technique:  I love the angle and the cropping.  The corner at the top is cropped, but the bottom is not, so it is a bit too off-centered.  The black and white finish is great.  This is a little blurry, which probably happened during resizing.  You might want to sharpen your image before submitting next time.<br/> <br/>Overall: A creative and good approach to this challenge. I''m sure many of us can relate to the despair of losing trust in a loved one.  Thanks for sharing this wonderful image with all of us!</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB Challenge:  Definitely meets the challenge. I think it would have been a little more compelling with a tear or maybe more jagged writing.  Your writing looks so beautiful and "happy", not desperate. (that''s a backwards compliment) Technique:  I love the angle and the cropping.  The corner at the top is cropped, but the bottom is not, so it is a bit too off-centered.  The black and white finish is great.  This is a little blurry, which probably happened during resizing.  You might want to sharpen your image before submitting next time. Overall: A creative and good approach to this challenge. I''m sure many of us can relate to the despair of losing trust in a loved one.  Thanks for sharing this wonderful image with all of us!', '2003-03-05 16:55:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(229930, 12911, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>This is a gorgeous shot and one you should be proud of for your first shot.  For the future, it helps to know that the "challenge subject" is taken pretty seriously here, which is why you didn''t score higher (he looks to sweet it be in despair).  I hope you will continue with the challenges though - you have a wonderful eye, and the image is beautiful.</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB This is a gorgeous shot and one you should be proud of for your first shot.  For the future, it helps to know that the "challenge subject" is taken pretty seriously here, which is why you didn''t score higher (he looks to sweet it be in despair).  I hope you will continue with the challenges though - you have a wonderful eye, and the image is beautiful.', '2003-03-05 16:57:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(229936, 12841, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>Challenge:  I think this met the challenge great from your perspective.  Unfortuneately, there are alot of people who did not fully understand what the objects were, so they were a little confused.  Maybe including a little more of the cross would have helped.<br/> <br/>Technique:  The colors here are gorgeous and I personally like the cropping with the white space.  It''s a creative approach, and obviously well-thought out.  There may be just a little TOO much white space to the right.<br/> <br/>Overall:  A sensitive subject that was sure to get mixed reviews. It also has alot to do with the way people view the death of Christ - some would feel despair, some gratitude, some indifference.  Being a Christian myself, I appreciate you going out there and trying this.</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB Challenge:  I think this met the challenge great from your perspective.  Unfortuneately, there are alot of people who did not fully understand what the objects were, so they were a little confused.  Maybe including a little more of the cross would have helped. Technique:  The colors here are gorgeous and I personally like the cropping with the white space.  It''s a creative approach, and obviously well-thought out.  There may be just a little TOO much white space to the right. Overall:  A sensitive subject that was sure to get mixed reviews. It also has alot to do with the way people view the death of Christ - some would feel despair, some gratitude, some indifference.  Being a Christian myself, I appreciate you going out there and trying this.', '2003-03-05 17:01:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(229943, 13106, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE<br/> <br/>Challenge:  I''m not quite sure how this fit into the despair challenge?  I would love to know your reasoning, in case I am missing something.<br/> <br/>Technique:  Usually not a good idea to just photograph a page in a book, but I appreciate you explaining it.  Alot of the challenge is based on your own creativity, and you may have to worry about copyrights, etc as well.  It is also off centered and a bit blurry, probably because it is from a book.''<br/> <br/>Overall:  Not sure it fits the challenge, and I would definitely stick with your OWN work from here on out.</td>', 'CRITIQUE Challenge:  I''m not quite sure how this fit into the despair challenge?  I would love to know your reasoning, in case I am missing something. Technique:  Usually not a good idea to just photograph a page in a book, but I appreciate you explaining it.  Alot of the challenge is based on your own creativity, and you may have to worry about copyrights, etc as well.  It is also off centered and a bit blurry, probably because it is from a book.'' Overall:  Not sure it fits the challenge, and I would definitely stick with your OWN work from here on out.', '2003-03-05 17:04:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(229948, 12992, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>Challenge: Obviously meets the challenge perfectly. It''s a shot that does evoke a desperate situation, and it is so sad how we can ignore the needs of another human being.<br/> <br/>Technique:  I think the technique here is great.  I think it would have been even more compelling if you would have crouched down, to get the perspective of the person looking up at the crowd. The image is also very small, making it hard to see the details.  The black and white works great.<br/> <br/>Overall:  A great image that definitely meets the challenge and opens our eyes. Very well done.</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB Challenge: Obviously meets the challenge perfectly. It''s a shot that does evoke a desperate situation, and it is so sad how we can ignore the needs of another human being. Technique:  I think the technique here is great.  I think it would have been even more compelling if you would have crouched down, to get the perspective of the person looking up at the crowd. The image is also very small, making it hard to see the details.  The black and white works great. Overall:  A great image that definitely meets the challenge and opens our eyes. Very well done.', '2003-03-05 17:06:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(229966, 12886, 3942, '<td valign="top">Congrats on the win Gigi, you found the real despair out there. I tried to give my shot a message too, but it is only staged. I feel highly shocked and deeply moved by looking on your picture. I know, that such living conditions are existing in the world, but such a photo brings that powerful back to my mind.</td>', 'Congrats on the win Gigi, you found the real despair out there. I tried to give my shot a message too, but it is only staged. I feel highly shocked and deeply moved by looking on your picture. I know, that such living conditions are existing in the world, but such a photo brings that powerful back to my mind.', '2003-03-05 17:34:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(229968, 12770, 1041, '<td valign="top"><i> Critique continued ... </i> <br/> <br/><b>(5) Meeting the Challenge</b> <br/>This is where I got stuck with this.  To me, this photo does not say despair.  That might be partially because I like snow, but I think mostly I associated despair with a human reaction.  That might just be me though.  ... Duh, I just read through the comments.  Of course it is a car.  Once you see that you wonder how you didn''t realize before, but I was too much taking in by the details  :)  I think this is what hurt your score a bit, I bet it would''ve done much better in an abstract challenge.<br/> <br/><b>(6) My Opinion</b> <br/>Doesn''t quite meet the challenge in my mind, even though I now know what it is, but gets closer.  But the photo is a fantastic abstract.  Wonderfully done!<br/> <br/>Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review.<br/> <br/>Franziska.</td>', ' Critique continued ...   (5) Meeting the Challenge This is where I got stuck with this.  To me, this photo does not say despair.  That might be partially because I like snow, but I think mostly I associated despair with a human reaction.  That might just be me though.  ... Duh, I just read through the comments.  Of course it is a car.  Once you see that you wonder how you didn''t realize before, but I was too much taking in by the details  :)  I think this is what hurt your score a bit, I bet it would''ve done much better in an abstract challenge. (6) My Opinion Doesn''t quite meet the challenge in my mind, even though I now know what it is, but gets closer.  But the photo is a fantastic abstract.  Wonderfully done! Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review. Franziska.', '2003-03-05 17:39:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(229969, 12770, 1041, '<td valign="top"><b>A Comment From The Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/><b>First Impression</b> <br/>Wow, a wonderful abstract :)  I wonder what it is?  How large / small is it?<br/> <br/><b>(1) Composition / Content</b> <br/>I really like letting my eyes wander through this photo to explore the curves and texture.  You can see all sorts of things in there if you just look long enough.  One type of flowers comes to mind (can''t remember their name) or an eye.  It is definitely recognizable that I''m looking at a snow drift here once I''ve looked at the photo closer.  I still am not sure what''s sticking out of the snow.  Since I don''t know what that is, I have no real sense of scale, either.<br/> <br/><b>(2) Background</b> <br/>Perfect black background that wonderfully complements the snow.<br/> <br/><b>(3) Camera Work / Technical</b> <br/>Taking photos of snow is very hard and you did very well.  The snow is just a bit overexposed on the top, but you have a good tonal range throughout your photo.  The lighting and shadows add a lot and enhance some of the texture nicely.<br/> <br/><b>(4) Post-processing</b> <br/>Converted to b&amp;w just fine, no processing artifacts visible, which is good.  There are a couple of stray white spots (for example towards the bottom on the left).  Not sure what they are, but worth cloning out now that the challenge is over.<br/> <br/><i> Continued below ...</i></td>', 'A Comment From The Critique Club  First Impression Wow, a wonderful abstract :)  I wonder what it is?  How large / small is it? (1) Composition / Content I really like letting my eyes wander through this photo to explore the curves and texture.  You can see all sorts of things in there if you just look long enough.  One type of flowers comes to mind (can''t remember their name) or an eye.  It is definitely recognizable that I''m looking at a snow drift here once I''ve looked at the photo closer.  I still am not sure what''s sticking out of the snow.  Since I don''t know what that is, I have no real sense of scale, either. (2) Background Perfect black background that wonderfully complements the snow. (3) Camera Work / Technical Taking photos of snow is very hard and you did very well.  The snow is just a bit overexposed on the top, but you have a good tonal range throughout your photo.  The lighting and shadows add a lot and enhance some of the texture nicely. (4) Post-processing Converted to b&w just fine, no processing artifacts visible, which is good.  There are a couple of stray white spots (for example towards the bottom on the left).  Not sure what they are, but worth cloning out now that the challenge is over.  Continued below ...', '2003-03-05 17:40:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(230066, 12811, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comments by Grayce<br/>This is a passionate image, very emotive.  I like the perspective from which you shot.<br/>The time of day you shot this worked very well, as the lighting is very nice and adds to the drama. Although the bottom is in shadow, the highlights on the face bring out the expression very well.<br/>There is very little color in this, and it looks good.  It''s just the right amount, in the sky, to contrast the statue. <br/>I like the way the subject is framed by the bare tree branches.<br/>Focus, and exposure are good.<br/>No digital noise.  <br/>Over all very good job!<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique Club Comments by GrayceThis is a passionate image, very emotive.  I like the perspective from which you shot.The time of day you shot this worked very well, as the lighting is very nice and adds to the drama. Although the bottom is in shadow, the highlights on the face bring out the expression very well.There is very little color in this, and it looks good.  It''s just the right amount, in the sky, to contrast the statue. I like the way the subject is framed by the bare tree branches.Focus, and exposure are good.No digital noise.  Over all very good job!Regards, Grayce', '2003-03-05 20:54:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(230068, 12902, 806, '<td valign="top">Excellent work, Sonifo. Sorry about the background on it. :0( I know this took alot of guts to do.</td>', 'Excellent work, Sonifo. Sorry about the background on it. :0( I know this took alot of guts to do.', '2003-03-05 20:55:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(230070, 13026, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club COmments by Grayce<br/>This is a very simple composition, and I like that.  Not much to clutter and distract.<br/>The lighting is interesting.  Soft, and somehow old-fashioned feeling to it.  Because the light is directional and properly placed on the subject, leaving the background dark and mysterious, this is very effective. <br/>The jar lid looks old, like the money had been saved there for a long time, and at last must be spent.  Scraping the proverbial barrel.  <br/>Spending the last of ones resources will cause despair.<br/>Even the wrinkled black cloth works well here. <br/>Composition is good, exposure, and color are also good.  This has the look of an old painting.<br/>Very good job.  In case you can''t tell, I really like this! Also you met the challenge wonderfully.<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique Club COmments by GrayceThis is a very simple composition, and I like that.  Not much to clutter and distract.The lighting is interesting.  Soft, and somehow old-fashioned feeling to it.  Because the light is directional and properly placed on the subject, leaving the background dark and mysterious, this is very effective. The jar lid looks old, like the money had been saved there for a long time, and at last must be spent.  Scraping the proverbial barrel.  Spending the last of ones resources will cause despair.Even the wrinkled black cloth works well here. Composition is good, exposure, and color are also good.  This has the look of an old painting.Very good job.  In case you can''t tell, I really like this! Also you met the challenge wonderfully.Regards, Grayce', '2003-03-05 21:05:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(230347, 13091, 5250, '<td valign="top">I was stuck inside, so this was about the best I could do where I was for this challenge. I was going more for a mood, as I also do not find snowstorms to invoke dispair. But I thought the dark colours made the picture look a bit gloomy!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-03-06 09:24:29.</i></td>', 'I was stuck inside, so this was about the best I could do where I was for this challenge. I was going more for a mood, as I also do not find snowstorms to invoke dispair. But I thought the dark colours made the picture look a bit gloomy!', '2003-03-06 09:24:14.000000', '2003-03-06 09:24:29.000000', 0),
(230396, 12844, 4162, '<td valign="top">Just had a look at your shot, thought I better mention that you are supposed to take the picture, the week of the challenge!  Lucky you to go to India, I would love to go!</td>', 'Just had a look at your shot, thought I better mention that you are supposed to take the picture, the week of the challenge!  Lucky you to go to India, I would love to go!', '2003-03-06 10:57:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(230440, 12829, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Jimmy...<br/> <br/>This is an excellent photo... I''m not sure why it didn''t score better than it did.  I may be one of a minority who really enjoys nice black and white images like this one.  Reading through the comments you received, it seems that several people interpret this challenge in such a way that they feel the human element is a requirement.  I find it unfortunate that viewers don''t allow their mind to open up to different interpretations.  I agree that some of the shots in this challenge where the human element was represented are quite strong, but it''s not a reason to abandon alternative interpretations.<br/> <br/>I like the dark sky in this shot... that seems to enhance the sense of despair for me.  The overall exposure is pretty good also.  The sunlight is on the boat in such a way that it makes it stand out prominently in the scene.  <br/> <br/>This photo is definitely printworthy.  I hope you decide to make this one available for print on dpc in the near future :)  <br/> <br/>Keep up the good work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Jimmy... This is an excellent photo... I''m not sure why it didn''t score better than it did.  I may be one of a minority who really enjoys nice black and white images like this one.  Reading through the comments you received, it seems that several people interpret this challenge in such a way that they feel the human element is a requirement.  I find it unfortunate that viewers don''t allow their mind to open up to different interpretations.  I agree that some of the shots in this challenge where the human element was represented are quite strong, but it''s not a reason to abandon alternative interpretations. I like the dark sky in this shot... that seems to enhance the sense of despair for me.  The overall exposure is pretty good also.  The sunlight is on the boat in such a way that it makes it stand out prominently in the scene.   This photo is definitely printworthy.  I hope you decide to make this one available for print on dpc in the near future :)   Keep up the good work :) John Setzler', '2003-03-06 11:54:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(230492, 13020, 1041, '<td valign="top"><i>Continued from above ...</i> <br/> <br/><b>(4) Post-processing</b> <br/>The colors are really nice, and the border is just perfect for the picture.  No signs of over-sharpening or compression, you did a good job.<br/> <br/><b>(5) Meeting the Challenge</b> <br/>Yes, in your light-hearted way I can kind of see how it meets the challenge, although without the title I wouldn''t have made the connection, and even then it is definitely more of a humorous approach and a bit of a stretch.  <br/> <br/><b>(6) My Opinion</b> <br/>A really nice macro shot, simple but effective with just minor details that could be enhanced a bit.  I like it a lot.  I suspect this would''ve scored much higher in a different challenge.   Keep up the good work :)<br/> <br/>Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review.<br/> <br/>Franziska.</td>', 'Continued from above ...  (4) Post-processing The colors are really nice, and the border is just perfect for the picture.  No signs of over-sharpening or compression, you did a good job. (5) Meeting the Challenge Yes, in your light-hearted way I can kind of see how it meets the challenge, although without the title I wouldn''t have made the connection, and even then it is definitely more of a humorous approach and a bit of a stretch.   (6) My Opinion A really nice macro shot, simple but effective with just minor details that could be enhanced a bit.  I like it a lot.  I suspect this would''ve scored much higher in a different challenge.   Keep up the good work :) Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review. Franziska.', '2003-03-06 13:06:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(230493, 13020, 1041, '<td valign="top"><b>A Comment From The Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/><b>First Impression</b> <br/>I hate sewing!  :)  I do like the black/red color combination though, and totally understand the feeling!<br/> <br/><b>(1) Composition / Content</b> <br/>I like simple and effective photos like this one.  The thread follows the line of the material which leads the user''s eye through the photo nicely.  The crease in the material on the right ensures that the eye is not just lead out of the photo, too.  <br/> <br/><b>(2) Background</b> <br/>Very plain and simple black.  Often one of the most effective backgrounds to use for still life / set-up shots.  <br/> <br/><b>(3) Camera Work / Technical</b> <br/>My initial reaction looking at that photo was that I like the shallow DOF but would like just a tad more nonetheless, because the red material has such nice texture to add to the photo, and because the thread seems just a little bit soft,  but I realize that you used the max aperture that your camera allows already.   Would it have been possible to tilt the needle and thread to have it more parallel to the photographic plane and therefore in focus, too?  It''s not a biggie though, I was just wondering.  There''s a bit of glare on the needle that you could have toned down with a sheet or two of paper between your lightsource and the needle.<br/> <br/><i>Continued below ... </i></td>', 'A Comment From The Critique Club  First Impression I hate sewing!  :)  I do like the black/red color combination though, and totally understand the feeling! (1) Composition / Content I like simple and effective photos like this one.  The thread follows the line of the material which leads the user''s eye through the photo nicely.  The crease in the material on the right ensures that the eye is not just lead out of the photo, too.   (2) Background Very plain and simple black.  Often one of the most effective backgrounds to use for still life / set-up shots.   (3) Camera Work / Technical My initial reaction looking at that photo was that I like the shallow DOF but would like just a tad more nonetheless, because the red material has such nice texture to add to the photo, and because the thread seems just a little bit soft,  but I realize that you used the max aperture that your camera allows already.   Would it have been possible to tilt the needle and thread to have it more parallel to the photographic plane and therefore in focus, too?  It''s not a biggie though, I was just wondering.  There''s a bit of glare on the needle that you could have toned down with a sheet or two of paper between your lightsource and the needle. Continued below ... ', '2003-03-06 13:06:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(230946, 12845, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings From the Critique Club<br/>This is a nice photograph which unfortunately doesn''t really meet the challenge of despair. Unless it is the despair of the mother of the child who owned the bear''s empty nest syndrome!<br/>Focus excellent. Composition good but I think it would have been better if the bear had been lying down in a sort of abandoned position!<br/>Exposure The bear is slightly over exposed.<br/>But all in all a nice try and a bit more subtle than all the staged suicides.</td>', 'Greetings From the Critique ClubThis is a nice photograph which unfortunately doesn''t really meet the challenge of despair. Unless it is the despair of the mother of the child who owned the bear''s empty nest syndrome!Focus excellent. Composition good but I think it would have been better if the bear had been lying down in a sort of abandoned position!Exposure The bear is slightly over exposed.But all in all a nice try and a bit more subtle than all the staged suicides.', '2003-03-07 00:22:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(230959, 12905, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings From the Critique Club<br/>Congratulations on a very good composition. You have handled the technicalities of a creating this type of image very well!<br/>Focus: excellent for such a long exposure. I take it you used a tripod.<br/>Colour: Black and white was a very good choice, colour would have been too distracting in this image.<br/>Exposure: Very good.<br/>I am actually surprised that this image didn''t place higher, perhaps it was just too subtle for some of the voters.<br/></td>', 'Greetings From the Critique ClubCongratulations on a very good composition. You have handled the technicalities of a creating this type of image very well!Focus: excellent for such a long exposure. I take it you used a tripod.Colour: Black and white was a very good choice, colour would have been too distracting in this image.Exposure: Very good.I am actually surprised that this image didn''t place higher, perhaps it was just too subtle for some of the voters.', '2003-03-07 00:31:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(231049, 13073, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>What a lovely old house! Congratulations on seeing the potential of this old house and on seeing the other side of despair Hope!<br/>Composition:Generally good but the house is tilted a little so it  looks like it is tipping over.<br/>Focus: A little soft, but not sobad that it spoils the image. It looks like you were shooting in very poor light.<br/>To me the bright splash of the yellow daffodils is the Hope in your title.<br/>Keep shooting You have a good eye and creative imagination The technicallities will come with practice.<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubWhat a lovely old house! Congratulations on seeing the potential of this old house and on seeing the other side of despair Hope!Composition:Generally good but the house is tilted a little so it  looks like it is tipping over.Focus: A little soft, but not sobad that it spoils the image. It looks like you were shooting in very poor light.To me the bright splash of the yellow daffodils is the Hope in your title.Keep shooting You have a good eye and creative imagination The technicallities will come with practice.', '2003-03-07 03:37:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(231050, 12995, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings From the Critique Club<br/>Composition: Lovely Photo! Good idea, but a little too posed for my idea of despair!<br/>The black and white format is perfect for this image though. <br/>Kate reminds of the little match Girl of the fairy tales.<br/>Exposure: It could be a little more contrasty it is leaning a little toward being overexposed.<br/>I am also surprised that this didn''t place a bit higher! Keep up the good work!</td>', 'Greetings From the Critique ClubComposition: Lovely Photo! Good idea, but a little too posed for my idea of despair!The black and white format is perfect for this image though. Kate reminds of the little match Girl of the fairy tales.Exposure: It could be a little more contrasty it is leaning a little toward being overexposed.I am also surprised that this didn''t place a bit higher! Keep up the good work!', '2003-03-07 03:45:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(231261, 12949, 656, '<td valign="top">CC: Great use wide angle techniques getting really close to the animal, to good effect.   The extremely over exposed sky is also dramatic, turning the bare trees into skeletal hands, grasping at the corpse.  Overall a very dramatic and creepy entry and certainly has many aspects of despair.<br/> <br/>Technically it does seem a bit oversharpened, particularly in the way the fur ''stands out'' and the sepia sky is a bit distracting with the cloud in the upper left.  I think this is great and you should keep trying to follow your own ideas, even if maybe not everyone gets it every time.</td>', 'CC: Great use wide angle techniques getting really close to the animal, to good effect.   The extremely over exposed sky is also dramatic, turning the bare trees into skeletal hands, grasping at the corpse.  Overall a very dramatic and creepy entry and certainly has many aspects of despair. Technically it does seem a bit oversharpened, particularly in the way the fur ''stands out'' and the sepia sky is a bit distracting with the cloud in the upper left.  I think this is great and you should keep trying to follow your own ideas, even if maybe not everyone gets it every time.', '2003-03-07 14:22:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(231354, 12943, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC</b> Howdy STEINR <br/> <br/>Fits The Challenge-Yes, although the despair is not tragic, or depressed to a great extent here. <br/>Composition-Nice. I don''t know if you happened on this person or if you staged it. I do like it, the person and the stairs. <br/>Background-Good <br/>My Opinion-It''s a bit dark for my liking. Of course it goes with the over all mood of the shot/challenge. I''m not sure I''d of cropped off the feet. I like this shot, it''s unusual, and good to look at.  <br/></td>', 'CC Howdy STEINR  Fits The Challenge-Yes, although the despair is not tragic, or depressed to a great extent here. Composition-Nice. I don''t know if you happened on this person or if you staged it. I do like it, the person and the stairs. Background-Good My Opinion-It''s a bit dark for my liking. Of course it goes with the over all mood of the shot/challenge. I''m not sure I''d of cropped off the feet. I like this shot, it''s unusual, and good to look at.  ', '2003-03-07 17:07:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(231362, 12813, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC</b> Hello ebuelna <br/>Fits The Challenge-Yes. <br/>Composition-Okay, could of done without the top portion with the bright light on the left. &lt;blue area&gt;<br/>My Opinion-If your looking for a job, lost yours this is a real slice of despair! I like this shot and think it speaks volumes. the car is a reminder of insurance payments, loan payment and gas. It''s a fine shot but the composition needs work.<br/> <br/></td>', 'CC Hello ebuelna Fits The Challenge-Yes. Composition-Okay, could of done without the top portion with the bright light on the left. <blue area>My Opinion-If your looking for a job, lost yours this is a real slice of despair! I like this shot and think it speaks volumes. the car is a reminder of insurance payments, loan payment and gas. It''s a fine shot but the composition needs work. ', '2003-03-07 17:14:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(231396, 12865, 1360, '<td valign="top">I really like this.  I like the "blurry moon", and the wasteland appearance of everything.  Someone commented that it''d have been better without "the light" in the upper left corner.  I disagree.  This picture sas something "post apocalyptic" to me.</td>', 'I really like this.  I like the "blurry moon", and the wasteland appearance of everything.  Someone commented that it''d have been better without "the light" in the upper left corner.  I disagree.  This picture sas something "post apocalyptic" to me.', '2003-03-07 18:18:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(231466, 12891, 6922, '<td valign="top">Anyway, I''ve seen some of your work-----;) i''ll never gonna beat you..sigh</td>', 'Anyway, I''ve seen some of your work-----;) i''ll never gonna beat you..sigh', '2003-03-07 20:13:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(231467, 13023, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comments by Grayce<br/>I can only imagine the despair of losing one so young.  It seems to meet the challenge well.  Anyone would feel despair in this situation.  <br/>The composition is a little cluttered.  Seeing the other gravestones distracts the eyes.  The little thing down in the lower left corner, is not really identifiable, and adds to the clutter.<br/>Exposure is very good.  With such a shiny subject there is often glare.  You handled this very well.<br/>Color works well here.  The greenery and red flowers contrast the stone and snow effectively.<br/>The picture is good and clear, with no visible noise.<br/>Over all you did a good job.<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique Club Comments by GrayceI can only imagine the despair of losing one so young.  It seems to meet the challenge well.  Anyone would feel despair in this situation.  The composition is a little cluttered.  Seeing the other gravestones distracts the eyes.  The little thing down in the lower left corner, is not really identifiable, and adds to the clutter.Exposure is very good.  With such a shiny subject there is often glare.  You handled this very well.Color works well here.  The greenery and red flowers contrast the stone and snow effectively.The picture is good and clear, with no visible noise.Over all you did a good job.Regards, Grayce', '2003-03-07 20:24:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(231581, 13073, 6604, '<td valign="top">I like the bright yellow against the drab background.</td>', 'I like the bright yellow against the drab background.', '2003-03-08 01:59:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(231597, 12893, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - The single rose dying isn''t really the greatest representation of despair, but for purposes of discussion, we''ll say it is, for now.  The framing of the picture is pretty good.  There are several problems with this picture though.  The picture is so small, something I don''t recommend.  The background is not terribly attractive either.  There is something up in the lefthand corner that is pretty distracting.<br/> <br/>Technical - The flash ring around the subject is not a good thing to have.  The reflection on the table from the flash or other means of lighting is equally unsightly.  The picture is not very well focused and that''s one of the "don''ts" if you will of the DPC voters, sometimes even if it is on purpose.  It definitely would''ve benefitted you to take this photo with ample light where the flash was not necessary or if you didn''t use the flash, a tripod to hold the camera still.  With the shutter speed as slow as it is, it is possible that you didn''t use the flash and had other means of lighting.  I have to think that little if any post processing was done to enhance this image.  I think that the sharpen filter in Photoshop would''ve helped some, but not to the degree this photo needs to be sharpened.  The color levels seem pretty good for the rose.<br/> <br/>Overall - This is not a strong photo and was voted accordingly.  I strongly suggest using something for a background like a posterboard or a piece of cloth.  I''ve seen some excellent results from a grocery bag as a background.  The next thing to tackle is the lighting so that the flower is amply lit without the reflections off the table or a ring around the flower.  Sharpness and clarity of pictures is a must on this site the greater majority of the time.  Perhaps a tripod would have been good if you didn''t use one this time.  The shutter speed is pretty slow to try to successfully handhold the camera still enough.  <br/> <br/>I hope this critique is helpful to you.  Good luck in future challenges.  If you have any questions or concerns about this PM me if you want to clarify anything or have me further explain something.<br/> <br/>Inspzil</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - The single rose dying isn''t really the greatest representation of despair, but for purposes of discussion, we''ll say it is, for now.  The framing of the picture is pretty good.  There are several problems with this picture though.  The picture is so small, something I don''t recommend.  The background is not terribly attractive either.  There is something up in the lefthand corner that is pretty distracting. Technical - The flash ring around the subject is not a good thing to have.  The reflection on the table from the flash or other means of lighting is equally unsightly.  The picture is not very well focused and that''s one of the "don''ts" if you will of the DPC voters, sometimes even if it is on purpose.  It definitely would''ve benefitted you to take this photo with ample light where the flash was not necessary or if you didn''t use the flash, a tripod to hold the camera still.  With the shutter speed as slow as it is, it is possible that you didn''t use the flash and had other means of lighting.  I have to think that little if any post processing was done to enhance this image.  I think that the sharpen filter in Photoshop would''ve helped some, but not to the degree this photo needs to be sharpened.  The color levels seem pretty good for the rose. Overall - This is not a strong photo and was voted accordingly.  I strongly suggest using something for a background like a posterboard or a piece of cloth.  I''ve seen some excellent results from a grocery bag as a background.  The next thing to tackle is the lighting so that the flower is amply lit without the reflections off the table or a ring around the flower.  Sharpness and clarity of pictures is a must on this site the greater majority of the time.  Perhaps a tripod would have been good if you didn''t use one this time.  The shutter speed is pretty slow to try to successfully handhold the camera still enough.   I hope this critique is helpful to you.  Good luck in future challenges.  If you have any questions or concerns about this PM me if you want to clarify anything or have me further explain something. Inspzil', '2003-03-08 07:35:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(231607, 13014, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings Swash from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - Very unorthodox depiction of despair.  I''m not real sure this meets the challenge as it was intended, but we''ll say it does for the purposes of discussion.  The expression is the first thing I notice and its good for this challenge.  I''m a coffee-holic also so I can relate.  I''d be wrestling with the EMPTY cup though.  The background needs to be smoothed out, or not so brightly lit.  The bright light on the bottom left corner really makes the wrinkles in the background stand out.  The hand is also very brightly lit.  I think the intensity of the lighting on the subject''s face is pretty good, but it''s generally too bright on the left side of the picture where I''m guessing the lighting source was held.<br/> <br/>Technical - I think this photo was pretty well taken actually.  Most of the problems I feel are due to the lighting.  The exposure of the subject is pretty good though on the face and for this particular challenge a little on the dark side is probably beneficial.  Processing stuff doesn''t seem to be too much of an issue on this picture.<br/> <br/>Overall - A humor photo more than a despair one.  I think the lighting is the source of most of the other problems with this picture.  The background needs to be smoothed out a little more as well.  I''m not particularly fond of this photo for this challenge.  I think there are other challenges for which it would be more appropriate.  Good luck swash on future challenges.  I look forward to your comments. - Bob</td>', 'Greetings Swash from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - Very unorthodox depiction of despair.  I''m not real sure this meets the challenge as it was intended, but we''ll say it does for the purposes of discussion.  The expression is the first thing I notice and its good for this challenge.  I''m a coffee-holic also so I can relate.  I''d be wrestling with the EMPTY cup though.  The background needs to be smoothed out, or not so brightly lit.  The bright light on the bottom left corner really makes the wrinkles in the background stand out.  The hand is also very brightly lit.  I think the intensity of the lighting on the subject''s face is pretty good, but it''s generally too bright on the left side of the picture where I''m guessing the lighting source was held. Technical - I think this photo was pretty well taken actually.  Most of the problems I feel are due to the lighting.  The exposure of the subject is pretty good though on the face and for this particular challenge a little on the dark side is probably beneficial.  Processing stuff doesn''t seem to be too much of an issue on this picture. Overall - A humor photo more than a despair one.  I think the lighting is the source of most of the other problems with this picture.  The background needs to be smoothed out a little more as well.  I''m not particularly fond of this photo for this challenge.  I think there are other challenges for which it would be more appropriate.  Good luck swash on future challenges.  I look forward to your comments. - Bob', '2003-03-08 07:57:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(231608, 12941, 2221, '<td valign="top">Greeting from the Critique Club, JD :)<br/> <br/>Composition: A typical portrait format. Nothing wrong with that. The tiles in the background (and the wet hair of the child of course) make you recognize that the photo was taken while bathing. The viewer can''t relate that to the despair theme and it makes the photo look like a private snapshot. So in my opinion the tiles are distracting.<br/> <br/>Lighting: In my opinion that''s the weakest part about this photo. Normal flashlight almost always sucks. Parts of the childs face are overexposured and there is a nasty shadow behind him (or her?) on the wall. This also makes the photo look too much like a snapshot for the family album. But I understand that this is a candid photo and you can''t really take a long time to prepare your lighting. But still, it doesn''t look nice to me. Black&amp;White was a good decision though.<br/> <br/>Focus: The grain is interesting. I like grain :) But I think to be truly effective you should increase the contrast and decrease brightness.<br/> <br/>Challenge: Well, the child itself looks more sad in general than despairing. But the title gives a good hint to understand your intent. I think it fits to the challenge.<br/> <br/>Creativity: A creative take on the challenge theme, to portray a childs helplessness to get its way. It sure is different from all the other photos.<br/></td>', 'Greeting from the Critique Club, JD :) Composition: A typical portrait format. Nothing wrong with that. The tiles in the background (and the wet hair of the child of course) make you recognize that the photo was taken while bathing. The viewer can''t relate that to the despair theme and it makes the photo look like a private snapshot. So in my opinion the tiles are distracting. Lighting: In my opinion that''s the weakest part about this photo. Normal flashlight almost always sucks. Parts of the childs face are overexposured and there is a nasty shadow behind him (or her?) on the wall. This also makes the photo look too much like a snapshot for the family album. But I understand that this is a candid photo and you can''t really take a long time to prepare your lighting. But still, it doesn''t look nice to me. Black&White was a good decision though. Focus: The grain is interesting. I like grain :) But I think to be truly effective you should increase the contrast and decrease brightness. Challenge: Well, the child itself looks more sad in general than despairing. But the title gives a good hint to understand your intent. I think it fits to the challenge. Creativity: A creative take on the challenge theme, to portray a childs helplessness to get its way. It sure is different from all the other photos.', '2003-03-08 07:58:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(231610, 13042, 2221, '<td valign="top">Arnit, this is my favourite of this challenge. I was a bit surprised that this photo was yours. But now I have even more respect for you because I see that you not only can make good photos with a "nice" theme but also about a more dark theme. The lighting is so great and focus, too.<br/> <br/>Stephan</td>', 'Arnit, this is my favourite of this challenge. I was a bit surprised that this photo was yours. But now I have even more respect for you because I see that you not only can make good photos with a "nice" theme but also about a more dark theme. The lighting is so great and focus, too. Stephan', '2003-03-08 08:19:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(231613, 12895, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - Wow that''s some serious snow!!  I live in Michigan and we get our fair share too.  We haven''t had a lot this year, but it has been exceptionally cold.  Oh wait, I''m doing a critique....sorry forgot.<br/> <br/>The subject matter in this particular photo does not lend itself very well to the despair challenge.  I think the snowy situation is inconvenient, but its difficult to judge despair without a more animate subject.  In any challenge, I think this subject doesn''t have the "WOW" factor we are constantly seeking.  It''s a little to busy in the background to be totally effective.  The picture is pretty dark and I have mixed feelings about that.  I think that it does add to the despair mood, but that it takes away from the overall picture quality.<br/> <br/>Technical - Maybe the contrast could be boosted a little so it wasn''t quite so grey.  The photo seems to be pretty well focused.  It does however seem a little underexposed.   Snow shots are difficult to properly expose.  A little higher EV and I think you could get the dreary feeling without being quite so dark.  By turning the contrast up a little with the current photo, I think you''d see about the same effect.<br/> <br/>Overall - Not a particularly good photo for this challenge.  I think the photo is a little dark overall but not badly taken.  Hope this was helpful for you.  GOod luck in future challenges. - Inspzil</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - Wow that''s some serious snow!!  I live in Michigan and we get our fair share too.  We haven''t had a lot this year, but it has been exceptionally cold.  Oh wait, I''m doing a critique....sorry forgot. The subject matter in this particular photo does not lend itself very well to the despair challenge.  I think the snowy situation is inconvenient, but its difficult to judge despair without a more animate subject.  In any challenge, I think this subject doesn''t have the "WOW" factor we are constantly seeking.  It''s a little to busy in the background to be totally effective.  The picture is pretty dark and I have mixed feelings about that.  I think that it does add to the despair mood, but that it takes away from the overall picture quality. Technical - Maybe the contrast could be boosted a little so it wasn''t quite so grey.  The photo seems to be pretty well focused.  It does however seem a little underexposed.   Snow shots are difficult to properly expose.  A little higher EV and I think you could get the dreary feeling without being quite so dark.  By turning the contrast up a little with the current photo, I think you''d see about the same effect. Overall - Not a particularly good photo for this challenge.  I think the photo is a little dark overall but not badly taken.  Hope this was helpful for you.  GOod luck in future challenges. - Inspzil', '2003-03-08 08:34:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(231764, 13077, 527, '<td valign="top">Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen.<br/> <br/>First of all, I like this concept quite a bit. However I am not especially happy with the composition. I do not feel that the way the pole is positioned between the people in the background as well as the close proximity of everyone lends itself well to the concept. What I would have tried is to position the pole and the young man along the left edge and closer to the camera and the background people more to the right edge, allowing for some distance between them. As it is now, most of the foreground space is not being effectively used and could be better used to visually convey the sense of separation that you intended. By reframing the scene you should be able to eliminate the distracting wall edge on the left side or show more of it and use it as a compositional element.<br/> <br/>I have no particular issues with the lighting. I think you handled that pretty well. I would not have guessed that it was taken in a nearly black room. There is a nice powerful tonal range though I feel that the darkest areas, such as the young man''s shirt could be even darker. The choice of black and white was a good one as it supports the isolation theme very well.<br/> <br/>The location with it''s openness and subtle textures was very appropriate for this image. I almost get the feeling that you could get lost and really feel alone in a building like this.<br/> <br/>This photo certainly meets the theme quite well and is properly executed with clean sharp lines.  Nice job.<br/> <br/>Tim</td>', 'Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen. First of all, I like this concept quite a bit. However I am not especially happy with the composition. I do not feel that the way the pole is positioned between the people in the background as well as the close proximity of everyone lends itself well to the concept. What I would have tried is to position the pole and the young man along the left edge and closer to the camera and the background people more to the right edge, allowing for some distance between them. As it is now, most of the foreground space is not being effectively used and could be better used to visually convey the sense of separation that you intended. By reframing the scene you should be able to eliminate the distracting wall edge on the left side or show more of it and use it as a compositional element. I have no particular issues with the lighting. I think you handled that pretty well. I would not have guessed that it was taken in a nearly black room. There is a nice powerful tonal range though I feel that the darkest areas, such as the young man''s shirt could be even darker. The choice of black and white was a good one as it supports the isolation theme very well. The location with it''s openness and subtle textures was very appropriate for this image. I almost get the feeling that you could get lost and really feel alone in a building like this. This photo certainly meets the theme quite well and is properly executed with clean sharp lines.  Nice job. Tim', '2003-03-08 16:54:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(231788, 12886, 6599, '<td valign="top">Thats mean to take a picture of a homeless person!</td>', 'Thats mean to take a picture of a homeless person!', '2003-03-08 18:08:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(231796, 13094, 527, '<td valign="top">Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen<br/> <br/>I think we have all been there. This is an absolutely fantastic shot. I''m not sure what, of significance, I would change in this photo. In my mind, I think I had even more books. : ) <br/> <br/>The lighting using the single point source is perfect. You have used it to highlight only what is neccessary which is exactly what you want in an effective shot. The choice of black and white was very appropriate as was choosing a dark sweatshirt to help make the face and hands stand out more.<br/> <br/>The composition is used to maximum effect. The arrangement of the books serve to frame the person and focus our attention on her. One idea that came to mind was to include many little bookmarks or tabs sticking out of several of the books to enforce the idea that they are actually being used and studied from as apposed to being merely props in a scene. The arrangement also gives me the impression that some sense of order is being attempted but at any moment the may be scattered in a fit of frustration.<br/> <br/>The real beauty is in how you showed only enough visual information to create a very impactful image. This clearly meets the challenge and you executed it to perfection. Great job.<br/> <br/>Tim</td>', 'Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen I think we have all been there. This is an absolutely fantastic shot. I''m not sure what, of significance, I would change in this photo. In my mind, I think I had even more books. : )  The lighting using the single point source is perfect. You have used it to highlight only what is neccessary which is exactly what you want in an effective shot. The choice of black and white was very appropriate as was choosing a dark sweatshirt to help make the face and hands stand out more. The composition is used to maximum effect. The arrangement of the books serve to frame the person and focus our attention on her. One idea that came to mind was to include many little bookmarks or tabs sticking out of several of the books to enforce the idea that they are actually being used and studied from as apposed to being merely props in a scene. The arrangement also gives me the impression that some sense of order is being attempted but at any moment the may be scattered in a fit of frustration. The real beauty is in how you showed only enough visual information to create a very impactful image. This clearly meets the challenge and you executed it to perfection. Great job. Tim', '2003-03-08 18:57:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(231835, 12937, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>I think you have a good idea, and I like that it gives the feeling of lost love, or love gone by.  I think the overall picture strength could have been increased by having the stems going diagonally, from lower left to upper right, instead of horizontally.  Horizontal, though it gives a stable feeling, also makes pictures feel static and almost stagnant.  Also, though cropping part of the subject out of a picture can be effective at times, here it feels almost accidental.  Maybe if the rose was placed within the frame a little differently, and not so close to the upper left edge.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>I think the limited depth of field hurt you on this one.  If you wanted a soft focus, you may have been better having the whole thing with a soft focus.  As it is, the eyes want ot rest on the rose, but the thorns are what is in focus, and it is conflicting to look at.  The lighting is awesome.  Very dreamy and emotional.<br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>I think you have an awesome idea here, there were just a couple of things in the execution that kept people from getting the full effect of you shot.  <br/> <br/> <br/></td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONI think you have a good idea, and I like that it gives the feeling of lost love, or love gone by.  I think the overall picture strength could have been increased by having the stems going diagonally, from lower left to upper right, instead of horizontally.  Horizontal, though it gives a stable feeling, also makes pictures feel static and almost stagnant.  Also, though cropping part of the subject out of a picture can be effective at times, here it feels almost accidental.  Maybe if the rose was placed within the frame a little differently, and not so close to the upper left edge. TECHNIQUEI think the limited depth of field hurt you on this one.  If you wanted a soft focus, you may have been better having the whole thing with a soft focus.  As it is, the eyes want ot rest on the rose, but the thorns are what is in focus, and it is conflicting to look at.  The lighting is awesome.  Very dreamy and emotional. OVERALL EFFECTI think you have an awesome idea here, there were just a couple of things in the execution that kept people from getting the full effect of you shot.    ', '2003-03-08 21:37:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(231926, 13056, 4159, '<td valign="top">Thank you for your critique, John, I was really pleased that this photograph was appreciated the way I intended.<br/> <br/>Martin</td>', 'Thank you for your critique, John, I was really pleased that this photograph was appreciated the way I intended. Martin', '2003-03-09 00:25:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(231938, 12886, 2420, '<td valign="top">i dont think that its mean to take a picture of a homeless person as long as the reason is genuinely to let the world know the harsh realities of what we do to our children. a ten for me gigi. congrats!</td>', 'i dont think that its mean to take a picture of a homeless person as long as the reason is genuinely to let the world know the harsh realities of what we do to our children. a ten for me gigi. congrats!', '2003-03-09 01:56:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(232003, 12879, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - The composition of this photo is awesome.  I really think this is a terrific idea and a great study of the human form.  It also reminds me of something on the Twilight Zone TV show from days of yore.  The hands over the back of the neck are great.  The greyscale with totally black background build the mood masterfully.  I wouldn''t change a thing.<br/> <br/>Technical - The soft focus works well here.  This is not a photo that demands an ultra sharp perspective to be effective.  I think this is where your score suffered a little, unfairly I might add.  The lighting is really really good.  The only thing that I might suggest for this pic at all is that the contrast be turned up a little to make the skin a little whiter and not so grey.<br/> <br/>Overall - I think you''ve really done an excellent photo.  I''m trying to think of reasons it didn''t score better and I really can''t think of anything except that it just doesn''t immediately grab the viewer, but it has definitely grown on me.  Great image and good luck in future challenges.  - Bob</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - The composition of this photo is awesome.  I really think this is a terrific idea and a great study of the human form.  It also reminds me of something on the Twilight Zone TV show from days of yore.  The hands over the back of the neck are great.  The greyscale with totally black background build the mood masterfully.  I wouldn''t change a thing. Technical - The soft focus works well here.  This is not a photo that demands an ultra sharp perspective to be effective.  I think this is where your score suffered a little, unfairly I might add.  The lighting is really really good.  The only thing that I might suggest for this pic at all is that the contrast be turned up a little to make the skin a little whiter and not so grey. Overall - I think you''ve really done an excellent photo.  I''m trying to think of reasons it didn''t score better and I really can''t think of anything except that it just doesn''t immediately grab the viewer, but it has definitely grown on me.  Great image and good luck in future challenges.  - Bob', '2003-03-09 08:46:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(232007, 13102, 3320, '<td valign="top">I have been assigned this picture by the critic club and have been looking at it everyday but I keep going back to the same thing :<br/>- it''s not despair to me, not at all<br/>- it''s an other cat<br/> <br/>The photograph commented him/herself that the forepaw is not in focus and the cough fabric too much in focus. that would have been my 2 comments in addition to the fact that that fabric is not appropriate at all for a cat picture I think. <br/> <br/>Final Impression : I do not think it was an appropriate picture for ''despair''  and , to me, it''s just a "cute cat".<br/> <br/>Lionel <br/>for the critique club<br/> <br/> <br/></td>', 'I have been assigned this picture by the critic club and have been looking at it everyday but I keep going back to the same thing :- it''s not despair to me, not at all- it''s an other cat The photograph commented him/herself that the forepaw is not in focus and the cough fabric too much in focus. that would have been my 2 comments in addition to the fact that that fabric is not appropriate at all for a cat picture I think.  Final Impression : I do not think it was an appropriate picture for ''despair''  and , to me, it''s just a "cute cat". Lionel for the critique club  ', '2003-03-09 10:35:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(232107, 12795, 3599, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>This is interesing take on the despair challenge.  Seems like an orginal idea to me which is a good thing.  I think there really isn''t much wrong with this photo.  I like the B&amp;W and framing.  I really think you did very good with this challenge and look forward to your photos for future challenges.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club. This is interesing take on the despair challenge.  Seems like an orginal idea to me which is a good thing.  I think there really isn''t much wrong with this photo.  I like the B&W and framing.  I really think you did very good with this challenge and look forward to your photos for future challenges.', '2003-03-09 16:23:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(232138, 12790, 2829, '<td valign="top">A visit from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>A very interesting photo. I think the biggest problem here is the lack of primary subject. I''m not sure exactly what the emphasis is. Are you emphasizing the withered grave stones or some other aspect of the photograph?<br/> <br/>I like the exposure. It''s dead on. Perhaps composition could be a bit better but that could be just because you have so much in the photo.</td>', 'A visit from the Critique Club :) A very interesting photo. I think the biggest problem here is the lack of primary subject. I''m not sure exactly what the emphasis is. Are you emphasizing the withered grave stones or some other aspect of the photograph? I like the exposure. It''s dead on. Perhaps composition could be a bit better but that could be just because you have so much in the photo.', '2003-03-09 18:59:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(232140, 12886, 806, '<td valign="top">What is mean? Would we rather all turn our eyes from the truth? Photojournalism at it''s best. This is so poignant. I feel for this child and all the others he represents.<br/>It makes me incredibly sad. I feel despair that I cannot help them.<br/></td>', 'What is mean? Would we rather all turn our eyes from the truth? Photojournalism at it''s best. This is so poignant. I feel for this child and all the others he represents.It makes me incredibly sad. I feel despair that I cannot help them.', '2003-03-09 19:20:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(232165, 13080, 6536, '<td valign="top">I like the sephia tone. It has good cropping. I would maybe add a little more contrast to the image.  Not sure if this would fit for despair. Overall nice shot.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'I like the sephia tone. It has good cropping. I would maybe add a little more contrast to the image.  Not sure if this would fit for despair. Overall nice shot.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 20:46:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(232168, 13051, 6536, '<td valign="top">Good picture for despair. It has great contrast &amp; cropping. Good shot.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Good picture for despair. It has great contrast & cropping. Good shot.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 20:49:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(232171, 13017, 6536, '<td valign="top">Nice theme. You might want to try this in black &amp; white I think it will have more effect.  Nice shot.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Nice theme. You might want to try this in black & white I think it will have more effect.  Nice shot.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 20:54:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(232172, 12975, 6536, '<td valign="top">Good despair shot.  It has good cropping &amp; contrast. Nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Good despair shot.  It has good cropping & contrast. Nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 20:57:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(232177, 12856, 6536, '<td valign="top">Very creative theme. It has very nice lighting &amp; cropping. Nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Very creative theme. It has very nice lighting & cropping. Nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:05:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(232182, 13008, 6536, '<td valign="top">Good despair shot. Nice composition. It has good contrast &amp; cropping. Good job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Good despair shot. Nice composition. It has good contrast & cropping. Good job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:07:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(232185, 12768, 6536, '<td valign="top">Good despair shot. Good cropping &amp; colors. Nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Good despair shot. Good cropping & colors. Nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:10:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(232186, 12789, 6536, '<td valign="top">Make a very strong statement. Good despair shot. I would maybe crop off a little to the left. Nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Make a very strong statement. Good despair shot. I would maybe crop off a little to the left. Nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:12:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(232188, 12773, 4162, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/>Composition-Content<br/>Great idea, but I think that you probably lost a lot of people with the meaning of it, one of those times when seeing the photographers comments at the time of voting would have helped! Perhaps a different angle of the sign would have added more interest.<br/>Technical<br/>The true colour of snow has come out well here, nice and clean! Lighting is a little dark, perhaps a different time of day would have been good, or even B/W and then more contrast would have provided a stonger impact.<br/>My Opinion<br/>Great idea and after reading your comments, I can see how this fits the challenge, just needs to be a little more obvious for this site! Good Luck in the next challenge.</td>', 'Critique ClubComposition-ContentGreat idea, but I think that you probably lost a lot of people with the meaning of it, one of those times when seeing the photographers comments at the time of voting would have helped! Perhaps a different angle of the sign would have added more interest.TechnicalThe true colour of snow has come out well here, nice and clean! Lighting is a little dark, perhaps a different time of day would have been good, or even B/W and then more contrast would have provided a stonger impact.My OpinionGreat idea and after reading your comments, I can see how this fits the challenge, just needs to be a little more obvious for this site! Good Luck in the next challenge.', '2003-03-09 21:14:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(232189, 12883, 6536, '<td valign="top">Good Theme. Nice cropping. Maybe try different lighting or in black &amp; white. Nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Good Theme. Nice cropping. Maybe try different lighting or in black & white. Nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:15:19.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(232190, 12947, 6536, '<td valign="top">Good angle &amp; has nice lighting. Good despair shot. You might want to try this in black &amp; white. Nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Good angle & has nice lighting. Good despair shot. You might want to try this in black & white. Nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:18:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(232197, 13070, 4162, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/>Composition=content<br/>Composition is lovely here, perhaps a little too much like Shiizzams recent win, but works well. You have made a good statement here, but it could be taken as too general to really fit the topic of despair.<br/>Technical<br/>Focus appears to be on the material, adding a bit of softness to the eye area, perhaps this could be sharper. I think that the flash is a bit too bright for this kind of shot, perhaps some soft side lighting would have been good, lighting up the eye area, but de-emphasising the material.<br/>My Opinion<br/>I like the shot, it has a meassge and does make people think. The composition is great.</td>', 'Critique ClubComposition=contentComposition is lovely here, perhaps a little too much like Shiizzams recent win, but works well. You have made a good statement here, but it could be taken as too general to really fit the topic of despair.TechnicalFocus appears to be on the material, adding a bit of softness to the eye area, perhaps this could be sharper. I think that the flash is a bit too bright for this kind of shot, perhaps some soft side lighting would have been good, lighting up the eye area, but de-emphasising the material.My OpinionI like the shot, it has a meassge and does make people think. The composition is great.', '2003-03-09 21:23:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(232198, 13013, 6536, '<td valign="top">You feel like your trapped in a room covered with ice. Very creative idea.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'You feel like your trapped in a room covered with ice. Very creative idea.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:25:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(232290, 12897, 2279, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Critique:<br/> <br/>First off I want to say I really liked this picture. Nice composition and a crisp clean image. I like how the texture of his clothes give a soft feeling to this shot. Why is he propping his elbow up with his hand? He does look sad though which gives a good sense of despair. It looks like its cold outside since his collar is propped up. Overall good detail, good lighting and good overall shot.</td>', 'Critique Club Critique: First off I want to say I really liked this picture. Nice composition and a crisp clean image. I like how the texture of his clothes give a soft feeling to this shot. Why is he propping his elbow up with his hand? He does look sad though which gives a good sense of despair. It looks like its cold outside since his collar is propped up. Overall good detail, good lighting and good overall shot.', '2003-03-09 22:40:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(232294, 13057, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Nice focus and sharpness, meets the challenge with a bit of a stretch, peanut or whatever hurts it.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>You have really great focus here, especially through the fence like that.  I think the thing that hurts you here is two-fold. First whatever he is eating is a big distraction against his dark fur.  Second, Although it is a great shot, I''m not sure it realy shows despair.<br/> <br/><b>Background</b> <br/>Background is excellent, the shallow DOF really makes this light background work well behind him.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>Focus and contrast are really super.<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical </b> <br/>I don''t know if you had the entire top paw before you cropped this or not but it would be nice to see all of it. The shot leans very slightly to the right. If you would have rotated it, the fence could have been lined up with cropping and the subject wouldn''t have really been affected.<br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge </b> <br/>Perhaps a bit of a stretch but fits the challenge. <br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I originally scored this shot a six which was higher than the majority of voters. I think it would have scored much higher in a different challenge. Good job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  Nice focus and sharpness, meets the challenge with a bit of a stretch, peanut or whatever hurts it. Composition/ Content  You have really great focus here, especially through the fence like that.  I think the thing that hurts you here is two-fold. First whatever he is eating is a big distraction against his dark fur.  Second, Although it is a great shot, I''m not sure it realy shows despair. Background Background is excellent, the shallow DOF really makes this light background work well behind him. Camera Work - Technical Focus and contrast are really super. Digital Processing - Technical  I don''t know if you had the entire top paw before you cropped this or not but it would be nice to see all of it. The shot leans very slightly to the right. If you would have rotated it, the fence could have been lined up with cropping and the subject wouldn''t have really been affected. Fits The Challenge  Perhaps a bit of a stretch but fits the challenge.  My Opinion On The Photo  I originally scored this shot a six which was higher than the majority of voters. I think it would have scored much higher in a different challenge. Good job, and keep up the good work.   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz ', '2003-03-09 22:46:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(232300, 12844, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Interesting shot, a bit of a stretch to meet the challenge, leaning a bit to the left.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>Everything in the shot seems to be leaning to the lower left.  I don''t know if your camera was a little crooked or if this was a hill but even the lion looks like it is leaning a little. The lion is really dead center in the shot as well.  It may have worked a little better if she was higher or lower.<br/> <br/><b>Background</b> <br/>I don''t think you had a lot of choice here. Next time ask the lion to get up and move against a dark background!!  LOL<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>Focus seems good but not real sharp and contrast is pretty good for the difference in light and shadow you have here.  I mentioned the camera tilt earlier.<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical </b> <br/>You may have tried upping the saturation just a little here.  It is probably pretty close to real color but a little bump would have made it more vibrant.<br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge </b> <br/>I feel that the think that hurt your score the most was not really feeling great despair here.  <br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>One of things that bothers me is the fact that the shot was apparently taken outside of the time allowed in the rules.  All shots have to be taken the week of the challenge.  I think that it meets the challenge with a bit of a stretch.  Good job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  Interesting shot, a bit of a stretch to meet the challenge, leaning a bit to the left. Composition/ Content  Everything in the shot seems to be leaning to the lower left.  I don''t know if your camera was a little crooked or if this was a hill but even the lion looks like it is leaning a little. The lion is really dead center in the shot as well.  It may have worked a little better if she was higher or lower. Background I don''t think you had a lot of choice here. Next time ask the lion to get up and move against a dark background!!  LOL Camera Work - Technical Focus seems good but not real sharp and contrast is pretty good for the difference in light and shadow you have here.  I mentioned the camera tilt earlier. Digital Processing - Technical  You may have tried upping the saturation just a little here.  It is probably pretty close to real color but a little bump would have made it more vibrant. Fits The Challenge  I feel that the think that hurt your score the most was not really feeling great despair here.   My Opinion On The Photo  One of things that bothers me is the fact that the shot was apparently taken outside of the time allowed in the rules.  All shots have to be taken the week of the challenge.  I think that it meets the challenge with a bit of a stretch.  Good job, and keep up the good work.   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz ', '2003-03-09 22:57:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(232303, 12870, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Corey, Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Beautiful church, meets the challenge, I like the Sepia feel.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>I like this shot a lot.  The way the light is hitting the person in the rear of the church is great although I''m not sure that didn''t hurt your score as the light almost shows the opposite of despair. I like the framing too.<br/> <br/><b>Background</b> <br/>The way the back wall is lit is excellent but once again takes away from the person which is really the focal point for this challenge.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>Focus seems good and contrast is excellent for the difference in light and shadow you have here. I don''t know whether you made it Sepia in camera or in Post Processing but it is the right choice especially for the challenge. <br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical </b> <br/>Everything seems good.<br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge </b> <br/>If there was more of a focus on the person, perhaps closer showing grief or praying, I think this would have done better.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I originally scored this shot a seven. I think that it meets the challenge well but can see why everyone wouldn''t think so. Good job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/></td>', 'Corey, Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  Beautiful church, meets the challenge, I like the Sepia feel. Composition/ Content  I like this shot a lot.  The way the light is hitting the person in the rear of the church is great although I''m not sure that didn''t hurt your score as the light almost shows the opposite of despair. I like the framing too. Background The way the back wall is lit is excellent but once again takes away from the person which is really the focal point for this challenge. Camera Work - Technical Focus seems good and contrast is excellent for the difference in light and shadow you have here. I don''t know whether you made it Sepia in camera or in Post Processing but it is the right choice especially for the challenge.  Digital Processing - Technical  Everything seems good. Fits The Challenge  If there was more of a focus on the person, perhaps closer showing grief or praying, I think this would have done better. My Opinion On The Photo  I originally scored this shot a seven. I think that it meets the challenge well but can see why everyone wouldn''t think so. Good job, and keep up the good work.   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz ', '2003-03-09 23:06:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(232307, 13028, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Interesting composition, beautiful shot, color could be either more or less saturated.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>The biggest thing I notice is the fact that the shot is very "middle ground" on the color scale. It is almost grayscale or black and white. It seems that either more color or a true B &amp; W may have worked better. I like the way the flowers complement the razor wire, good eye there to see it. <br/> <br/><b>Background</b> <br/>Once again, the color thing.  A blue sky would have been so nice here in the background.  A true B &amp; W wouldn''t have made it necessary.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>Focus seems excellent especially on the wire. The wire does feel like it is moving down a bit to the right but I think that is actually the barbed wire under it.  A tough decision there.<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical </b> <br/>The only things I might have changed is to possibly crop it up just a little on the bottom, and I would have used a darker border.  Just my opinion.<br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge </b> <br/>Fits the challenge but a bit of a stretch.  I think the flowers kind of overpower the wire with a "Good over Evil" kind of message and that probably hurt your score some. <br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I originally scored this shot a seven which is on the high side of your scores. I think that it meets the challenge but could understand how everyone didn''t get it. Good job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  Interesting composition, beautiful shot, color could be either more or less saturated. Composition/ Content  The biggest thing I notice is the fact that the shot is very "middle ground" on the color scale. It is almost grayscale or black and white. It seems that either more color or a true B & W may have worked better. I like the way the flowers complement the razor wire, good eye there to see it.  Background Once again, the color thing.  A blue sky would have been so nice here in the background.  A true B & W wouldn''t have made it necessary. Camera Work - Technical Focus seems excellent especially on the wire. The wire does feel like it is moving down a bit to the right but I think that is actually the barbed wire under it.  A tough decision there. Digital Processing - Technical  The only things I might have changed is to possibly crop it up just a little on the bottom, and I would have used a darker border.  Just my opinion. Fits The Challenge  Fits the challenge but a bit of a stretch.  I think the flowers kind of overpower the wire with a "Good over Evil" kind of message and that probably hurt your score some.  My Opinion On The Photo  I originally scored this shot a seven which is on the high side of your scores. I think that it meets the challenge but could understand how everyone didn''t get it. Good job, and keep up the good work.   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz  ', '2003-03-09 23:17:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(232310, 12917, 3280, '<td valign="top">b]Greetings from the Critique Club };-) [/b]<br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Pretty composition, meets the challenge, feels backwards.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>Well focused shot with good color but I think that everything draws the eye to the pinecone and the arrangement and away from the urn which needs to be your focal point to meet the challenge.<br/> <br/><b>Background</b> <br/>The black background works well. A more somber cloth may have worked better, especially the patch of bright blue behind the arrangement.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>Focus on the arrangement is good but once again the focal point of the urn is a bit soft.  It should probably be the other way around.<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical </b> <br/>The photo seems to be cropped a little too close. I feel as if I am missing something at the top of the shot.<br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge </b> <br/>Fits the challenge. <br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I originally scored this shot a six which is on the high end of your score. I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a little to finish even higher than it did. Good job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/>BTW, I lost my Dad last year and my Mom two years before that.  I sure do miss them, sorry for your loss.<br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/></td>', 'b]Greetings from the Critique Club };-) [/b] Initial thoughts  Pretty composition, meets the challenge, feels backwards. Composition/ Content  Well focused shot with good color but I think that everything draws the eye to the pinecone and the arrangement and away from the urn which needs to be your focal point to meet the challenge. Background The black background works well. A more somber cloth may have worked better, especially the patch of bright blue behind the arrangement. Camera Work - Technical Focus on the arrangement is good but once again the focal point of the urn is a bit soft.  It should probably be the other way around. Digital Processing - Technical  The photo seems to be cropped a little too close. I feel as if I am missing something at the top of the shot. Fits The Challenge  Fits the challenge.  My Opinion On The Photo  I originally scored this shot a six which is on the high end of your score. I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a little to finish even higher than it did. Good job, and keep up the good work.  BTW, I lost my Dad last year and my Mom two years before that.  I sure do miss them, sorry for your loss.  I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz ', '2003-03-09 23:26:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(232316, 12933, 2667, '<td valign="top">NDS, This is fine, fine shot. I especially love the tone you have achieved. Nicely done!</td>', 'NDS, This is fine, fine shot. I especially love the tone you have achieved. Nicely done!', '2003-03-09 23:41:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(234551, 12902, 714, '<td valign="top">Thanks everyone for your comments. It did better then I thought it would.  <br/> <br/>Thanks Karen and Karmat.  It was a very tough week for me.  I will see him someday.  God has blessed me with 4 other wonderful children.</td>', 'Thanks everyone for your comments. It did better then I thought it would.   Thanks Karen and Karmat.  It was a very tough week for me.  I will see him someday.  God has blessed me with 4 other wonderful children.', '2003-03-11 00:57:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(235388, 13014, 1005, '<td valign="top">Normally, I send out thank you notes after each challenge.  Taking the time to comment should always be rewarded.  I just couldn''t do it for this one.  It was a silly picture, granted.  An attempt to lighten what was certain to be a very depressing challenge subject.  <br/>I will continue to ask - Concentrate on the QUALITY of the photo and it''s attempt to meet the challenge.  Set up, props and studio type lighting are "nice", but the idea of the site is to improve photo quality.  Is this a quality photo?  Does it attempt to meet the challenge?  nuf said!</td>', 'Normally, I send out thank you notes after each challenge.  Taking the time to comment should always be rewarded.  I just couldn''t do it for this one.  It was a silly picture, granted.  An attempt to lighten what was certain to be a very depressing challenge subject.  I will continue to ask - Concentrate on the QUALITY of the photo and it''s attempt to meet the challenge.  Set up, props and studio type lighting are "nice", but the idea of the site is to improve photo quality.  Is this a quality photo?  Does it attempt to meet the challenge?  nuf said!', '2003-03-11 19:48:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(236021, 12917, 1005, '<td valign="top">Carsten - I had hoped this would place higher for you.  I liked it very much.  At times, I think the general viewing audience forgets that life isn''t set up in advance for your photographic advantage.  You did very well and under the circumstances, this is an incredibly well taken photo.  I took a few shots during my father''s funeral, but I was crying so hard, it was difficult to concentrate on taking the photo.  There was also quite a bit of social expectations/pressure/confusion (in my mind), to not look like I was having fun (taking pictures) during my Dad''s ceremony.  A very difficult time.  My heart goes out to you.</td>', 'Carsten - I had hoped this would place higher for you.  I liked it very much.  At times, I think the general viewing audience forgets that life isn''t set up in advance for your photographic advantage.  You did very well and under the circumstances, this is an incredibly well taken photo.  I took a few shots during my father''s funeral, but I was crying so hard, it was difficult to concentrate on taking the photo.  There was also quite a bit of social expectations/pressure/confusion (in my mind), to not look like I was having fun (taking pictures) during my Dad''s ceremony.  A very difficult time.  My heart goes out to you.', '2003-03-12 13:05:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(237354, 13042, 472, '<td valign="top">this should have done WAYYY better</td>', 'this should have done WAYYY better', '2003-03-14 16:53:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(238867, 13039, 6213, '<td valign="top">Wow! You must have done a lot of shoveling to take this picture seeing how it snowed 25 inches in DC a few days before you took this photo.</td>', 'Wow! You must have done a lot of shoveling to take this picture seeing how it snowed 25 inches in DC a few days before you took this photo.', '2003-03-17 12:00:45.000000', NULL, 0),
